window.B79_BANK1X = [
{"id":"d1I55x6","tp":"line","d":["2023-05"],"q":"The graph below shows pollution levels in London between 1600 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Pollution levels in London, 1600–2000 (micrograms per cubic metre)","unit":"µg/m³","x":["1600","1650","1700","1750","1800","1850","1900","1950","2000"],"series":[{"n":"Sulphur dioxide","v":[200,400,680,650,780,900,800,350,20]},{"n":"Smoke","v":[60,100,165,180,240,360,420,250,10]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường tại các mốc 50 năm nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the levels of two pollutants, sulphur dioxide and smoke, in London's air between 1600 and 2000, measured in micrograms per cubic metre.\n\nOverall, both pollutants rose for well over two centuries, peaked in the second half of the nineteenth century and then fell steeply to negligible levels. Sulphur dioxide concentrations were at least twice as high as smoke concentrations for most of the period.\n\nIn 1600, sulphur dioxide stood at about 200 micrograms per cubic metre. It more than tripled to 680 by 1700 and, after a slight dip in the mid-eighteenth century, climbed to a peak of around 900 in 1850. Smoke levels, by contrast, increased far more gradually, from approximately 60 micrograms in 1600 to 180 in 1750, before accelerating to a high of 420 by 1900.\n\nFrom the late nineteenth century onwards, both figures declined rapidly, fluctuating sharply during the first half of the twentieth century. Sulphur dioxide fell to 350 micrograms by 1950, while smoke dropped to 250. By 2000, each had fallen to 20 micrograms or less, lower than at any earlier point on the graph.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1c3wU","tp":"line","d":["2023-05"],"q":"The graph below shows the number of overseas visitors who came to the UK for three different purposes between 1989 and 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Overseas visitors to the UK by purpose of visit, 1989–2011 (millions)","unit":"Millions","x":["1989","1991","1994","1996","1999","2001","2004","2006","2009","2011"],"series":[{"n":"Business","v":[6.5,6.5,8,9,8,7,6.5,7,8,9]},{"n":"Holiday","v":[3.5,4,4.3,4.5,5,5.5,6,6.5,7,6]},{"n":"Meeting friends and relatives","v":[5,4,4.5,6,6.5,6.5,7,7.5,8,7]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; tên nước được ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu và mốc năm là xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many overseas visitors came to the UK for business, for holidays and to meet friends and relatives between 1989 and 2011.\n\nOverall, visitor numbers in all three categories were higher at the end of the period than at the beginning. Business was the most common reason for visiting in almost every year, while holidays were generally the least common, although the gap between the three narrowed.\n\nIn 1989, 6.5 million people visited on business. This figure rose to a peak of 9 million in 1996, after which it fell back to 6.5 million in 2004 and then climbed again, returning to 9 million by 2011. These fluctuations meant that business travel was briefly overtaken in 2004 by visits to friends and relatives.\n\nThe other two categories followed a steadier upward path. Visits to friends and relatives dipped from 5 million to 4 million in 1991 but then doubled to 8 million in 2009, whereas holiday visits grew continuously from 3.5 million to 7 million over the same twenty years. Both figures then fell by 1 million in the final two years.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d187EB2","tp":"line","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The graph shows the number of visitors to four international museums between 1980 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Visitors to four international museums, 1980–2015 (millions)","unit":"Millions","x":["1980","1985","1990","1995","2000","2005","2010","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Louvre","v":[7.8,8,8.8,9,8.4,8.6,8.9,9.5]},{"n":"Vatican Museums","v":[4.8,5.1,5.8,6.8,7.4,8,8.7,9.2]},{"n":"London Science Museum","v":[4.1,4.1,3.2,3,2.9,2.6,2.5,2.2]},{"n":"Shenzhen Museum","v":[3.8,3.6,3.3,3.2,3,3.4,3.6,3.8]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many people visited four museums, the Louvre, the Vatican Museums, the London Science Museum and the Shenzhen Museum, between 1980 and 2015.\n\nOverall, the Louvre attracted the most visitors throughout, although the Vatican Museums grew far faster and had almost caught up by 2015. The other two museums received far fewer visitors, and the London Science Museum was the only one whose attendance fell.\n\nIn 1980, the Louvre welcomed 7.8 million visitors, compared with 4.8 million at the Vatican Museums. The Louvre's figure rose to 9 million in 1995, dipped slightly and then recovered, reaching 9.5 million in 2015. Attendance at the Vatican Museums, by contrast, increased in every five-year period and almost doubled to 9.2 million.\n\nThe London Science Museum and the Shenzhen Museum both started at about 4 million. Visits to the former declined steadily to 2.2 million, roughly half the original figure, while the latter slipped to 3 million in 2000 before returning to 3.8 million, exactly where it had begun.","lv":"ex","pn":["Louvre","Vatican","Shenzhen"]},
{"id":"d19hhrT","tp":"line","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The graph below shows relative price changes for fresh fruits and vegetables, sugars and sweets, and carbonated drinks between 1978 and 2009. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Relative price changes, 1979–2009 (index)","unit":"Index","x":["1979","1981","1983","1985","1987","1989","1991","1993","1995","1997","1999","2001","2003","2005","2007","2009"],"series":[{"n":"Fresh fruits and vegetables","v":[65,75,100,107,117,143,175,180,212,215,247,250,268,288,315,328]},{"n":"Carbonated drinks","v":[65,80,95,105,110,122,135,143,150,160,164,175,182,190,203,211]},{"n":"Sugar and sweets","v":[65,85,97,105,110,113,128,133,135,147,153,157,160,165,176,197]},{"n":"Consumer-price index","v":[70,82,98,105,108,108,114,116,118,121,118,126,127,130,138,155]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường (theo hình trong bộ đề) nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows relative changes in the prices of fresh fruit and vegetables, sugar and sweets, and carbonated drinks between 1979 and 2009, alongside the general consumer-price index.\n\nOverall, all four measures rose over the thirty years, although the price of fresh fruit and vegetables increased far more than the others. The prices of the two sweetened products also outpaced the consumer-price index, which showed the smallest rise.\n\nIn 1979, all four indices stood at a similar level, between 65 and 70, and they rose together to just over 100 by 1985. From that point they diverged. The index for fresh fruit and vegetables climbed steeply, with minor fluctuations, to 175 in 1991 and 250 in 2001, before ending at 328 in 2009, roughly five times its starting value.\n\nThe other three lines rose much more gently. Carbonated drinks increased steadily to 211 in 2009 and sugar and sweets to 197, each roughly tripling over the period. The consumer-price index, by contrast, remained between 105 and 130 from 1985 until 2005 and finished at only 155, less than half the figure for fresh produce.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1o2PRP","tp":"line","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The chart below gives information about how many European citizens of various age groups frequently went to the gym from 1990 to 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Europeans who frequently went to the gym, by age group, 1990–2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1990","1994","1998","2002","2006","2010"],"series":[{"n":"Aged 44–53","v":[16.5,23,20,22,23.5,25.5]},{"n":"Aged 34–43","v":[15.5,18.5,16,20,25,17.5]},{"n":"Aged 24–33","v":[7,7,8.7,9,9.5,10]},{"n":"Aged 18–23","v":[5,5,6.8,6.5,6.3,6.2]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph illustrates the proportion of Europeans in four age groups who went to the gym regularly between 1990 and 2010.\n\nOverall, gym attendance was considerably more common among the two older groups than among the two younger ones, and the oldest group finished the period with the highest figure. Attendance rose in every age group, although the increase among people in their late teens and early twenties was marginal.\n\nIn 1990, roughly 16% of the oldest group, aged 44 to 53, were regular gym users, slightly more than among those aged 34 to 43. The older group's figure jumped to 23% in 1994, dipped four years later and then climbed steadily, reaching just over 25% in 2010. The second-oldest group followed a similar pattern until 2006, when it peaked at 25% and briefly took the lead, before falling sharply to 17.5%.\n\nBy contrast, the figures for the two youngest groups never exceeded 10%. Attendance among people aged 24 to 33 grew gradually from 7% to that level over the two decades, while the share of 18-to-23-year-olds was the lowest throughout, edging up from 5% to just over 6%.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1yLRh5","tp":"line","d":["2023-02"],"q":"The graph below shows the number of enquiries received by the Tourist Information Office in one city over a six-month period in 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Enquiries received by a Tourist Information Office, January–June 2011","x":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun"],"series":[{"n":"In person","v":[450,600,800,1220,1550,1900]},{"n":"By letter / email","v":[750,700,700,560,350,350]},{"n":"By telephone","v":[900,800,1000,1000,1400,1600]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many enquiries a city's Tourist Information Office received in person, by telephone and by letter or email during the first six months of 2011.\n\nOverall, enquiries made in person and by telephone rose considerably, whereas written enquiries declined. Face-to-face enquiries grew the fastest, moving from the least to the most common method.\n\nIn January, the telephone was the most popular channel, with about 900 enquiries, followed by letters and emails (750) and personal visits (450). In-person enquiries then increased every month, overtaking written enquiries in February and telephone calls in March, and reached 1,900 in June, more than four times the January figure.\n\nTelephone enquiries dipped to 800 in February and stood at around 1,000 in March and April, before climbing to 1,600 by June. By contrast, the number of letters and emails stayed close to 700 until March and then halved, levelling off at 350 in May and June, less than a fifth of the figure for personal visits.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1PN2oh","tp":"line","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The graph below shows the percentage of workers from five different European countries that were absent for a day or more due to illness from 1991 to 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Workers absent through illness in five European countries, 1991–2001 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1991","1992","1993","1994","1995","1996","1997","1998","1999","2000","2001"],"series":[{"n":"Netherlands","v":[4.9,5.6,5.3,5.0,4.8,4.0,4.4,4.7,5.0,5.2,5.4]},{"n":"France","v":[5.0,5.0,4.3,3.9,3.5,3.1,3.0,3.0,2.9,2.9,2.9]},{"n":"Sweden","v":[3.0,3.1,3.0,3.1,3.0,3.1,3.5,4.0,4.3,4.4,4.6]},{"n":"UK","v":[2.5,2.4,2.5,2.4,2.5,2.4,2.6,2.4,2.5,2.4,2.6]},{"n":"Germany","v":[1.4,1.6,1.4,1.6,1.5,1.7,1.6,1.8,1.6,1.8,1.5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph compares the proportion of employees in five European countries who took at least one day off work through illness each year from 1991 to 2001.\n\nOverall, absence rates were highest in the Netherlands for almost the entire period and lowest in Germany. While the figures for the UK and Germany were stable, France saw a marked decline and Sweden a marked rise in the second half of the decade.\n\nIn 1991, about 5% of workers in both the Netherlands and France were absent through illness. The Dutch figure peaked at 5.6% the following year, fell to a low of 4% in 1996 and then recovered steadily to 5.4% in 2001. The French rate, by contrast, dropped continuously to 3% by 1997 and stayed just under that level for the rest of the period.\n\nSweden's rate hovered around 3% until 1996, after which it climbed every year to reach 4.6%, overtaking France and finishing second only to the Netherlands. The UK and Germany recorded the lowest figures throughout, fluctuating narrowly around 2.5% and 1.5% respectively.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1oChaG","tp":"line","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The graph below shows the number of overseas visitors to three different areas in a European country between 1987 and 2007. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Overseas visitors to three areas in a European country, 1987–2007 (thousands)","unit":"Thousands","x":["1987","1992","1997","2002","2007"],"series":[{"n":"The coast","v":[40,35,48,62,75]},{"n":"The mountains","v":[20,25,30,31,34]},{"n":"The lakes","v":[10,25,40,75,50]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many overseas tourists visited three areas of one European country, the coast, the mountains and the lakes, between 1987 and 2007.\n\nOverall, all three areas attracted more foreign visitors at the end of the period than at the beginning. The coast was the most popular destination in most years, whereas the lakes saw by far the greatest growth, despite a sharp fall after 2002.\n\nIn 1987, the coast received 40,000 overseas visitors, twice as many as the mountains and four times as many as the lakes. After dipping slightly to 35,000 in 1992, the coastal figure climbed steadily, reaching 75,000 in 2007. Visitor numbers in the mountains also rose, although far more slowly, from 20,000 to just under 35,000.\n\nThe lakes followed a very different pattern. Having started at only 10,000, the number of visitors there matched the mountain figure in 1992 and then surged to a peak of 75,000 in 2002, briefly overtaking the coast. It subsequently fell by a third to 50,000, which was still five times the original figure.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1ON2EF","tp":"line","d":["2022-12"],"q":"The graph below shows US government spending on research in five fields (health, space, energy, general science and other) between 1980 and 2008. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"US government spending on research, 1980–2008 ($ billions)","unit":"$ billion","x":["1980","1984","1988","1992","1996","2000","2004","2008"],"series":[{"n":"Health","v":[8.7,8,10.5,13.2,15.2,16.8,22,18]},{"n":"Space","v":[6.2,5.1,5.2,5.5,8,8.7,8.9,9.6]},{"n":"Energy","v":[5,5.1,5,5.2,8.5,5.1,5.1,7.8]},{"n":"General science","v":[2.5,2.7,4,3.3,3.9,5,5,5]},{"n":"Other","v":[7.9,5,5.3,6.5,8,8.7,8,5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how much the US government spent on research in five areas, namely health, space, energy, general science and other fields, between 1980 and 2008.\n\nOverall, health received by far the most funding throughout the period and was the only area in which spending grew substantially. Expenditure on the remaining four fields stayed within a much narrower range, although space and general science both finished higher than they had started.\n\nHealth research cost approximately $8.7 billion in 1980. After a slight dip in the early 1980s, the figure climbed steadily to almost $17 billion in 2000 and then surged to a peak of $22 billion in 2004, before falling back to $18 billion, still roughly twice the amount spent on any other field.\n\nSpending on space and on other research followed a similar path until 2000, rising from around $5 billion each in 1984 to nearly $9 billion. Thereafter, space edged up to $9.6 billion, whereas the other category dropped back to $5 billion. Energy stayed at about $5 billion in most years, apart from brief spikes to $8.5 billion in 1996 and almost $8 billion in 2008. General science, the least funded area, doubled from $2.5 billion to $5 billion by 2000 and then levelled off.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d15Yrtp","tp":"line","d":["2022-09"],"q":"The chart below shows the changes that took place in three different areas of crime in Newport city centre from 2003 to 2012. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Number of incidents of three types of crime in Newport city centre, 2003–2012","x":["2003","2004","2005","2006","2007","2008","2009","2010","2011","2012"],"series":[{"n":"Burglary","v":[3400,3700,3100,2600,1600,1200,1400,1300,1500,1300]},{"n":"Car theft","v":[2750,2800,2750,2100,2250,2100,2300,2600,2500,2300]},{"n":"Robbery (theft from the person)","v":[700,800,850,600,550,500,650,700,600,650]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how the number of burglaries, car thefts and robberies in Newport city centre changed between 2003 and 2012.\n\nOverall, burglary fell substantially, while car theft and robbery ended the period only slightly below their starting levels. Consequently, car theft replaced burglary as the most common of the three crimes, and robbery was by far the least common throughout.\n\nIn 2003, there were about 3,400 burglaries, a figure that rose to a peak of 3,700 the following year. Burglary then dropped steeply, falling below car theft in 2007 and reaching a low of 1,200 in 2008, roughly a third of its peak. It fluctuated between 1,300 and 1,500 for the rest of the period.\n\nCar theft, by contrast, was far more stable. It stood at around 2,750 until 2005, fell to 2,100 in 2006 and then recovered to 2,600 in 2010, before easing to 2,300, which was almost twice the burglary figure. Robbery never exceeded 850 incidents a year, peaking at that level in 2005 and fluctuating between 500 and 700 thereafter.","lv":"ex","rc":1,"pn":["Newport"]},
{"id":"d1sfg8W","tp":"line","d":["2022-03","2022-09"],"q":"The graph below shows the percentage of people accessing news from four sources from 1995, with projections to 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"People accessing news from four sources, 1995–2025 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1995","2000","2010","2020","2025"],"series":[{"n":"TV","v":[70,58,60,55,50]},{"n":"Newspaper","v":[57,50,45,32,29]},{"n":"Radio","v":[52,50,43,29,22]},{"n":"Internet","v":[0,2,29,40,56]}],"note":"Số liệu năm 2025 là dự báo. Các mốc năm trên trục ngang không cách đều nhau."},"mo":"The line graph shows the proportion of people who obtained news from television, newspapers, radio and the internet between 1995 and 2020, together with a projection for 2025.\n\nOverall, the three traditional sources all lost part of their audience, whereas the internet grew from nothing to become a major source of news and is projected to overtake television by 2025.\n\nIn 1995, television was the most popular source, used by 70% of people, followed by newspapers (57%) and radio (52%). Television's share fell to 58% in 2000 but declined only slightly afterwards, standing at 55% in 2020, and it is projected to reach 50% five years later. Newspapers and radio lost ground more quickly: both dropped to around 30% in 2020, and they are predicted to decline further, to 29% and 22% respectively.\n\nThe internet, by contrast, was not used for news at all in 1995 and by only 2% of people in 2000. Its share then rose sharply to 29% in 2010 and 40% in 2020, overtaking both newspapers and radio, and it is forecast to climb to 56%, the highest figure of any source.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1t6aNA","tp":"line","d":["2022-05"],"q":"The graph below shows the average monthly salary in Japan and the prices of black-and-white and colour televisions between 1953 and 1983. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Average monthly salary and television prices in Japan, 1953–1983 (thousand yen)","unit":"Thousand yen","x":["1953","1960","1963","1973","1983"],"series":[{"n":"Price of colour TV","v":[null,100,91,61,47]},{"n":"Price of black-and-white TV","v":[109,48,38,26,20]},{"n":"Salary","v":[18,21,22.5,29.5,66]}],"note":"Đường giá TV màu bắt đầu từ khoảng năm 1960. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ; các mốc năm không cách đều."},"mo":"The line graph shows the prices of black-and-white and colour televisions in Japan, together with the average monthly salary, between 1953 and 1983, all measured in thousands of yen.\n\nOverall, both types of television became far cheaper over the period, whereas salaries rose, most rapidly in the final decade. By 1983, the average monthly salary exceeded the price of either kind of set, having been well below both prices in earlier years.\n\nIn 1953, a black-and-white television cost 109,000 yen, roughly six times the monthly salary of 18,000 yen. Its price fell steeply to around 40,000 yen by the early 1960s and then declined more gradually, reaching 20,000 yen in 1983, less than a fifth of the original figure.\n\nColour televisions first appear on the graph in 1960 at 100,000 yen, more than twice the price of a black-and-white set at that time. Their price also fell, to 61,000 yen in 1973 and 47,000 yen a decade later. Salaries, by contrast, grew slowly to about 30,000 yen in 1973, overtaking the price of a black-and-white set, and then more than doubled to 66,000 yen, well above the price of a colour television.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1SynOg","tp":"line","d":["2022-04"],"q":"The graph below shows global demand for four types of textile fibre (polyester, cotton, cellulosic and wool) between 1980 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Global demand for textile fibres, 1980–2015 (million tonnes)","unit":"million tonnes","x":["1980","1985","1990","1995","2000","2005","2010","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Polyester","v":[5,7,11,20,32,48,58,68]},{"n":"Cotton","v":[13,18,18,20,25,26,28,32]},{"n":"Cellulosic","v":[3,3,2.5,2,3,5.5,7.5,9.5]},{"n":"Wool","v":[2,3,3.5,4.5,4,2,1.5,1.5]}],"note":"Hình đề không ghi đơn vị; đơn vị triệu tấn được ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows global demand for four types of textile fibre, polyester, cotton, cellulosic and wool, between 1980 and 2015, measured in millions of tonnes.\n\nOverall, demand for polyester grew more than tenfold and overtook cotton to become by far the most widely used fibre. Cotton consumption also rose, though far more slowly, whereas demand for cellulosic fibres and wool remained comparatively small throughout.\n\nIn 1980, cotton was the dominant fibre at 13 million tonnes, more than twice the figure for polyester (5 million). Demand for cotton climbed to 18 million tonnes by 1985, levelled off for several years and then increased gradually to 32 million in 2015. Polyester, by contrast, rose slowly at first but accelerated after 1990, drawing level with cotton at 20 million tonnes in 1995 and climbing to 68 million by the end of the period, more than double the cotton figure.\n\nDemand for the other two fibres never exceeded 10 million tonnes. Cellulosic fibres stayed at 2 to 3 million tonnes until 2000 and then tripled to 9.5 million, while wool edged up to 4.5 million in 1995 before falling back to 1.5 million.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1334c9","tp":"line","d":["2022-03"],"q":"The graph below shows the amount of money invested in four types of investment fund (property, gold, fine art and company shares) in the US between 1988 and 2014. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Investment funds in the US, 1988–2014 ($ million)","unit":"$ million","x":["1988","1990","1992","1994","1996","1998","2000","2002","2004","2006","2008","2010","2012","2014"],"series":[{"n":"Gold","v":[100,110,65,122,128,100,115,122,150,187,302,352,450,380]},{"n":"Fine art","v":[80,87,92,80,92,50,105,65,175,100,150,200,303,333]},{"n":"Company shares","v":[40,50,60,75,157,162,168,172,200,215,223,232,248,252]},{"n":"Property","v":[50,60,50,82,60,68,76,84,108,88,117,150,180,192]}],"note":"Hình đề không ghi đơn vị; đơn vị triệu đô la được ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how much money was invested in four types of fund in the United States, namely property, gold, fine art and company shares, every two years from 1988 to 2014.\n\nOverall, investment in all four categories grew considerably over the period, with the fastest growth occurring after 2006. Gold attracted the most money in the majority of years, while property was generally the least popular option.\n\nIn 1988, gold was the leading investment at $100 million, followed by fine art at $80 million, with property and company shares at $50 million or less. Gold remained between $65 million and roughly $130 million until 2002, then rose steeply to a peak of $450 million in 2012 before slipping to $380 million. Fine art was far more volatile, swinging between $50 million and $175 million up to 2006, after which it more than tripled to reach $333 million.\n\nCompany shares, by contrast, followed a much smoother path. Having more than doubled to almost $160 million in 1996, they stayed ahead of gold for a decade and then climbed gradually to just over $250 million. Property grew the most slowly, finishing at $192 million, almost four times its initial figure.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1tUpq2","tp":"line","d":["2021-11"],"q":"The graph below compares changes in the birth rates of China and the USA between 1920 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Birth rates of China and the USA, 1920–2000 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1920","1925","1930","1935","1940","1945","1950","1955","1960","1965","1970","1975","1980","1985","1990","1995","2000"],"series":[{"n":"China","v":[10.2,11.7,10,15,5,5,20,8.7,6.7,5.7,3.6,2.6,1.6,1.6,2.8,2.8,1.6]},{"n":"USA","v":[11.2,12.3,11,12.7,12,6.5,14.5,13,12.2,12.3,10.3,9.2,8.1,7,7.1,8.2,7]}],"note":"Hình đề không có chú giải; theo đề gốc, đường đạt đỉnh 20% năm 1950 là Trung Quốc. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph compares the birth rates of China and the USA over the eighty years from 1920 to 2000.\n\nOverall, both countries had considerably lower birth rates at the end of the period than at the beginning, despite sharp fluctuations before 1950. The decline was far steeper in China, whose rate was above the American one at its peak but well below it for the whole of the second half of the century.\n\nBetween 1920 and 1935, the two rates were similar, moving between 10% and 15%. Both then fell sharply: China's rate dropped to 5% in 1940 and stayed there for five years, while the American rate dropped to 6.5% in 1945. A sharp recovery followed, with China reaching a peak of 20% in 1950, the highest figure on the graph, and the USA a peak of 14.5%.\n\nAfter 1950, the trends diverged. China's birth rate collapsed to under 9% within five years and continued to fall, reaching a low of 1.6% in 1980 and finishing at the same level. The American rate, by contrast, declined far more gradually, remaining at around 12% until 1965 and ending the period at 7%, more than four times the Chinese figure.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1lOsI6","tp":"line","d":["2021-10","2018-12"],"q":"The graph below shows how people bought music from 2011 to 2018. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Percentage of total music sales by method, 2011–2018","unit":"%","x":["2011","2012","2013","2014","2015","2016","2017","2018"],"series":[{"n":"Streams","v":[5,7,10,15,20,28,35,41]},{"n":"Downloads","v":[35,38,41,43,40,37,34,30]},{"n":"CDs purchased","v":[55,49,42,36,32,28,26,25]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how the share of music sales accounted for by streaming, downloads and CDs changed between 2011 and 2018.\n\nOverall, CDs lost their position as the leading format, whereas streaming grew from a negligible share to become the most common way of buying music. Downloads rose at first but then declined, finishing slightly lower than they had started.\n\nIn 2011, CDs represented 55% of music sales, well ahead of downloads at 35%, while streaming made up only 5%. The share of CDs then fell continuously, dropping below that of downloads in 2013 and reaching 25% in 2018, less than half its original level.\n\nDownloads, by contrast, climbed to a peak of 43% in 2014, when they were the best-selling format, before slipping back to 30%. Streaming followed the opposite trajectory to CDs: its share doubled to 10% by 2013 and then accelerated, overtaking CDs in 2016 and downloads a year later. By 2018, it had reached 41%, roughly eight times the 2011 figure.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1qo6pV","tp":"line","d":["2021-07"],"q":"The graph shows information about the number of people in Australia who used Chinese, Arabic, Italian and Greek as their first language between 1986 and 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"People in Australia by first language, 1986–2011","x":["1986","1991","1996","2001","2006","2011"],"series":[{"n":"Chinese","v":[110000,157000,205000,255000,302000,350000]},{"n":"Arabic","v":[50000,70000,90000,110000,130000,150000]},{"n":"Italian","v":[150000,140000,130000,118000,107000,107000]},{"n":"Greek","v":[227000,218000,208000,199000,190000,180000]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many people in Australia spoke Chinese, Arabic, Italian and Greek as their first language between 1986 and 2011.\n\nOverall, the number of Chinese and Arabic speakers grew strongly, whereas the two European languages lost speakers. Consequently, Chinese rose from third place to become by far the most widely spoken of the four languages.\n\nIn 1986, Greek had the most native speakers, at roughly 227,000, followed by Italian with 150,000. Both figures then declined steadily: Greek fell to 180,000 by 2011, while Italian dropped to 107,000 in 2006 and stayed at that level.\n\nChinese, by contrast, started at 110,000 speakers and increased by about 50,000 every five years, overtaking Italian around 1990 and Greek in 1996, and reached 350,000 in 2011, more than three times its initial figure and almost twice the number of Greek speakers. Arabic grew at a slower but equally steady rate, tripling from 50,000 to 150,000 and overtaking Italian shortly after 2001.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d10ipme","tp":"line","d":["2021-06"],"q":"The graph shows information about the number of marriages in the UK from 1951 to 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Number of marriages in the UK, 1951–2001","x":["1951","1961","1971","1981","1991","2001"],"series":[{"n":"All marriages","v":[400000,395000,450000,425000,400000,375000]},{"n":"First marriages","v":[345000,350000,360000,345000,300000,250000]},{"n":"Remarriages","v":[150000,145000,145000,150000,160000,200000]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường (theo hình trong bộ đề) nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the number of marriages in the UK between 1951 and 2001, divided into first marriages and remarriages.\n\nOverall, the total number of marriages was slightly lower at the end of the period than at the beginning, having peaked in 1971. First marriages declined substantially after that year, whereas remarriages became more common.\n\nIn 1951, there were 400,000 marriages, of which roughly 345,000 were first marriages. The total was stable for a decade, rose to a peak of 450,000 in 1971 and then fell steadily, reaching 375,000 in 2001. First marriages followed a similar pattern at a lower level, edging up to 360,000 in 1971 before dropping by almost a third to 250,000 over the next thirty years.\n\nRemarriages, by contrast, stayed at about 150,000 a year until 1981. They then increased, slowly at first and more quickly in the 1990s, to reach 200,000 in 2001. Consequently, the gap between the two types of marriage narrowed from nearly 200,000 to just 50,000.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1mqvQr","tp":"line","d":["2021-04"],"q":"The graph below shows the proportion of total expenditure on five categories in a certain European country between 1960 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Share of total expenditure in a European country, 1960–2000 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1960","1970","1980","1990","2000"],"series":[{"n":"Food","v":[34,30,24,19.5,14]},{"n":"Leisure","v":[19,17,16,13,11]},{"n":"Clothing","v":[10,9,7,6,5]},{"n":"Transport","v":[9,10,12,14,15]},{"n":"Fuel / energy","v":[6,5,4.5,3.8,3]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows what proportion of total spending in one European country went on five categories, food, leisure, clothing, transport and fuel or energy, between 1960 and 2000.\n\nOverall, the share of spending devoted to four of the five categories declined, with food showing by far the steepest fall. Transport was the only category whose share grew, and by the end of the period it had become the largest item.\n\nIn 1960, food made up 34% of expenditure, almost twice the figure for leisure (19%). Both shares then fell steadily: food dropped by roughly five percentage points per decade to 14% in 2000, while leisure declined more gently to 11%. Clothing and fuel followed the same downward path at a lower level, halving from 10% to 5% and from 6% to 3% respectively.\n\nTransport, by contrast, rose in every decade. Starting at 9%, slightly below clothing, it climbed to 12% in 1980 and overtook leisure in the late 1980s, before reaching 15% in 2000, marginally above food.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1G7Ka","tp":"line","d":["2021-03"],"q":"The graph below gives information about the percentage of people living in Australia who were born in Asia, the UK, and other regions. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"People living in Australia who were born overseas, 1976–2011 (% of population)","unit":"%","x":["1976","1981","1986","1991","1996","2001","2006","2011"],"series":[{"n":"Total born overseas","v":[23,22.5,22,21.5,21,20.5,22.5,25]},{"n":"Born in the UK","v":[14,12,10,9,8,7,6,5]},{"n":"Born in other regions","v":[11,9,8,8.5,10,12,14,13]},{"n":"Born in Asia","v":[3,4,5,6,7,9,11.5,15]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows what percentage of Australia's population was born overseas, in Asia, the UK and other regions, between 1976 and 2011.\n\nOverall, the share of residents born in the UK fell steadily, whereas the share born in Asia rose throughout the period and eventually became the largest of the three. The total proportion of overseas-born residents declined slightly for twenty-five years before climbing to its highest level at the end.\n\nIn 1976, 14% of people living in Australia had been born in the UK, compared with only 3% in Asia. The two figures then moved in opposite directions: the UK-born share dropped in every five-year period, reaching 5% in 2011, while the Asian-born share grew gradually to 7% in 1996, overtook the UK figure shortly afterwards and accelerated to 15%.\n\nThe proportion born in other regions dipped from 11% to 8% in 1986, recovered to a peak of 14% in 2006 and ended at 13%. Taken together, these changes meant that the overall figure slipped from 23% to just over 20% in 2001, after which it rose sharply to 25%, or one in four residents.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1NzpKU","tp":"line","d":["2020-04"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of people of different age groups who went to the cinema once a month or more in one European country from 2000 to 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"People who went to the cinema once a month or more, by age group, 2000–2011 (%)","unit":"%","x":["2000","2002","2004","2006","2008","2010","2011"],"series":[{"n":"Aged 15–24","v":[15,25,33,34,37,52,52]},{"n":"Aged 7–14","v":[10,12,17,20,24,38,30]},{"n":"Aged 25–35","v":[3,7,11,17,18,32,30]},{"n":"Aged 35 and over","v":[1,1,3,4,6,10,13]}],"note":"Đường gốc dao động theo từng năm; bảng chỉ ghi các mốc chính. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the percentage of people in four age groups who went to the cinema at least once a month in one European country between 2000 and 2011.\n\nOverall, regular cinema attendance rose in every age group over the period. Young adults were the most frequent cinema-goers throughout, while the oldest group was by far the least likely to attend.\n\nIn 2000, 15% of people aged 15 to 24 went to the cinema monthly. Despite sharp fluctuations in the middle of the period, this figure more than tripled, standing at 52% in 2011. Children aged 7 to 14 formed the second most active group, with attendance rising from 10% to a peak of 38% in 2010, before slipping to 30%.\n\nThe 25–35 group started from a far lower base of 3%, but its figure grew faster than any other, reaching the same level as the children's group by the end of the period. By contrast, attendance among the oldest group stayed below 10% until 2010 and finished at just 13%, a quarter of the figure for young adults.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d15FYhB","tp":"line","d":["2020-03"],"q":"The graph below shows the percentage of people in different age groups in one city who attended music concerts between 2010 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"People who attended music concerts in one city, by age group, 2010–2015 (%)","unit":"%","x":["2010","2011","2012","2013","2014","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Aged 16–24","v":[40,55,59,60,63,70]},{"n":"Aged 25–44","v":[52,40,50,52,52,53]},{"n":"Aged 45–54","v":[40,32,30,32,42,48]},{"n":"Aged 55–64","v":[24,28,28,28,23,22]},{"n":"Aged 75+","v":[10,21,12,11,13,21]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the percentage of people in five age groups in one city who attended music concerts between 2010 and 2015.\n\nOverall, concert attendance was far more common among younger and middle-aged residents than among older ones. The youngest group recorded the strongest growth and became the most frequent concert-goers, whereas the figures for most other groups ended close to where they had started.\n\nIn 2010, people aged 25 to 44 had the highest attendance at 52%, while the youngest group (16 to 24) and the middle-aged group (45 to 54) both stood at 40%. The youngest group's figure then jumped sharply in 2011 and continued to rise, reaching 70% in 2015. Attendance in the second-youngest group dipped at first but recovered to its original level, and the middle-aged group fell to 30% before climbing to 48%.\n\nThe two oldest groups went to concerts far less often. Roughly a quarter of people aged 55 to 64 attended each year, with little variation, while the figure for those aged 75 and over fluctuated between 10% and 21%, finishing level with the group below it.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1sU1fD","tp":"line","d":["2019-07"],"q":"The graph below shows the percentage of Australian exports to four countries from 1990 to 2012. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Percentage of Australian exports to four countries, 1990–2012","unit":"%","x":["1990","1995","2000","2005","2010","2012"],"series":[{"n":"Japan","v":[26,20,18,18,17,17]},{"n":"US","v":[11,8,11,8,5,7]},{"n":"China","v":[2,3,5,11,25,28]},{"n":"India","v":[1,1,1,5,7,5]}],"note":"Các mốc năm trên trục ngang không cách đều nhau."},"mo":"The line graph shows what percentage of Australia's exports went to four countries, Japan, the US, China and India, between 1990 and 2012.\n\nOverall, China's share of Australian exports grew more than tenfold, making it the largest market by the end of the period, whereas the shares going to Japan and the US declined. India's share also rose but stayed relatively small.\n\nIn 1990, Japan was by far the leading destination, taking 26% of exports, more than twice the figure for the US (11%). Japan's share fell to 18% by 2000 and then levelled off at about 17%. The proportion sent to the US fluctuated between 8% and 11% until 2005, dropped to a low of 5% in 2010 and recovered slightly to 7%.\n\nChina, by contrast, received only 2% of Australian exports in 1990. Its share rose slowly to 5% in 2000, doubled over the next five years and then surged to 25% in 2010, overtaking Japan, before reaching 28% in 2012. India accounted for just 1% until 2000, after which its share climbed to a peak of 7% in 2010 and ended at 5%.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1ELVQa","tp":"line","d":["2019-05"],"q":"The graph below gives information about car ownership in the UK from 1975 to 2005. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Households with regular use of a car, Great Britain, 1975–2005 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1975","1985","1995","2005"],"series":[{"n":"1 car","v":[42,38,39.5,41]},{"n":"0 car","v":[30,23,19,18]},{"n":"2 cars","v":[7.5,12.5,15,17]},{"n":"3 or more cars","v":[2.5,4,6,7.5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the percentage of households in Great Britain with no car, one car, two cars and three or more cars between 1975 and 2005.\n\nOverall, one-car households formed the largest group throughout, and their share changed little. The proportion of households without a car fell markedly, whereas multiple-car ownership became steadily more common.\n\nIn 1975, 42% of households had regular use of one car. This figure dipped to 38% in 1985 and then recovered gradually, ending the period at 41%. Households with no car made up 30% of the total at the start, but their share dropped to 23% within a decade and continued to decline more slowly, reaching 18% in 2005.\n\nBy contrast, the figures for households owning more than one car rose in every decade. The share with two cars more than doubled from 7.5% to 17%, almost matching the no-car figure by 2005, while households with three or more cars tripled from 2.5% to 7.5%, although they were still by far the smallest group.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1kDyka","tp":"line","d":["2018-11"],"q":"The graph below shows UK acid rain emissions, measured in millions of tonnes, from four different sectors between 1990 and 2007. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"UK acid rain emissions by sector, 1990–2007 (million tonnes)","unit":"Million tonnes","x":["1990","1993","1996","1999","2002","2005","2007"],"series":[{"n":"Electricity, gas and water supply","v":[3.3,2.5,1.7,1.0,0.9,0.7,0.5]},{"n":"Other industries","v":[2.1,2.1,1.7,1.5,1.3,1.2,1.2]},{"n":"Transport and communication","v":[0.7,0.8,0.8,0.7,0.9,1.0,0.7]},{"n":"Domestic","v":[0.6,0.6,0.5,0.4,0.3,0.2,0.2]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề gốc. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ; các mốc năm không cách đều."},"mo":"The line graph shows how much acid rain gas was emitted by four sectors in the UK between 1990 and 2007, measured in millions of tonnes.\n\nOverall, total emissions fell considerably over the period. The decline was steepest in the electricity, gas and water supply sector, which went from being by far the largest source to one of the smallest, whereas emissions from transport and communication changed very little.\n\nIn 1990, electricity, gas and water supply produced 3.3 million tonnes, compared with 2.1 million from other industries. Emissions from the utilities sector then dropped rapidly, falling level with those of other industries in 1996 and reaching 1 million tonnes in 1999, before declining more slowly to 0.5 million in 2007, less than a sixth of the initial figure. Other industries also reduced their output, although more gradually, to 1.2 million tonnes.\n\nTransport and communication, by contrast, fluctuated between 0.7 and 1 million tonnes throughout and finished at its starting level. Domestic emissions were the lowest in every year, shrinking from 0.6 to 0.2 million tonnes.","lv":"pre","rc":1},
{"id":"d1g6K","tp":"line","d":["2018-01"],"q":"The line graph below shows the percentage of tourists to Scotland who visited four different attractions between 1980 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Percentage of tourists to Scotland who visited different attractions, 1980–2010","unit":"%","x":["1980","1985","1990","1995","2000","2005","2010"],"series":[{"n":"Aquarium","v":[20,35,20,23,20,15,9]},{"n":"Castle","v":[23,27,40,45,35,34,33]},{"n":"Zoo","v":[10,15,9,12,10,15,20]},{"n":"Festival","v":[30,27,25,24,23,20,18]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the percentage of tourists to Scotland who visited four attractions, the aquarium, the castle, the zoo and the festival, between 1980 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the castle became the most visited attraction and the zoo also gained popularity, whereas the aquarium and the festival attracted a smaller share of tourists at the end of the period than at the beginning.\n\nIn 1980, the festival was the leading attraction, drawing 30% of tourists, followed by the castle (23%) and the aquarium (20%). The castle's share then rose sharply to a peak of 45% in 1995, before settling at around a third of visitors from 2000 onwards. The festival, by contrast, declined gradually in every five-year period and finished at 18%.\n\nThe aquarium was the most volatile attraction. Its share jumped to 35% in 1985, making it briefly the most popular site, but then fell back and eventually dropped to just 9%, the lowest figure on the graph. The zoo, which had started at 10%, fluctuated until 2000 and then doubled to 20%, overtaking both the aquarium and the festival.","lv":"pre","rc":1},
{"id":"d1KvGNw","tp":"line","d":[],"q":"The graph below shows average carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions per person in the United Kingdom, Sweden, Italy and Portugal between 1967 and 2007. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Average carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions per person, 1967–2007 (metric tonnes)","unit":"tonnes","x":["1967","1977","1987","1997","2007"],"series":[{"n":"United Kingdom","v":[10.8,10.7,10.0,9.5,8.8]},{"n":"Sweden","v":[8.7,10.3,7.1,6.2,5.5]},{"n":"Italy","v":[4.3,6.3,6.7,7.7,7.7]},{"n":"Portugal","v":[1.2,2.3,3.3,5.3,5.4]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph compares average carbon dioxide emissions per person in four European countries, the United Kingdom, Sweden, Italy and Portugal, at ten-year intervals between 1967 and 2007.\n\nOverall, emissions per person fell in the UK and Sweden, whereas they rose in Italy and Portugal, so that the gap between the four countries narrowed considerably. Despite its decline, the UK remained the highest emitter throughout the period.\n\nIn 1967, the average Briton produced almost 11 tonnes of carbon dioxide, a figure that decreased gradually to 8.8 tonnes by 2007. Sweden's emissions initially climbed from 8.7 tonnes to a peak of 10.3 tonnes in 1977, almost matching the UK, but then dropped sharply to about 7 tonnes a decade later and continued to fall, reaching 5.5 tonnes in 2007.\n\nThe two southern European countries started from far lower levels. Italy's figure rose from 4.3 tonnes to 7.7 tonnes in 1997, overtaking Sweden's in the late 1980s, and then remained unchanged. Portugal recorded the lowest emissions in every year, although its figure increased more than fourfold, from just over 1 tonne in 1967 to 5.4 tonnes in 2007, by which time it was virtually level with Sweden's.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 11 · Test 3"},
{"id":"d1xFWiX","tp":"line","d":[],"q":"The graph below shows the number of tourists visiting a particular Caribbean island between 2010 and 2017. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Number of tourists visiting a Caribbean island, 2010–2017 (millions of visitors)","unit":"Millions","x":["2010","2011","2012","2013","2014","2015","2016","2017"],"series":[{"n":"Visitors staying on cruise ships","v":[0.25,0.5,0.25,0.5,1,1.25,1.5,2]},{"n":"Visitors staying on island","v":[0.75,0.75,1.25,1.5,1.5,1.5,1.25,1.5]},{"n":"Total","v":[1,1.25,1.5,2,2.5,2.75,2.75,3.5]}]},"mo":"The line graph shows how many tourists visited a particular Caribbean island each year from 2010 to 2017, distinguishing between those who stayed on the island and those who stayed on cruise ships.\n\nOverall, the total number of visitors rose more than threefold over the period. Most of this growth came from cruise passengers, who went from being a small minority to outnumbering visitors staying on the island.\n\nIn 2010, the island received 1 million tourists, three-quarters of whom stayed on the island itself. The number of island-based visitors doubled to 1.5 million by 2013 and then changed little, apart from a slight dip to 1.25 million in 2016.\n\nCruise-ship visitors, by contrast, fluctuated between 0.25 and 0.5 million until 2013, after which their number rose every year, reaching 2 million in 2017, eight times the initial figure. Having overtaken island stays in 2016, they made up well over half of all tourists in the final year. Consequently, the total climbed steadily to 2.75 million in 2015, levelled off for a year and then jumped to 3.5 million.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 15 · Test 2","pn":["Caribbean"]},
{"id":"d1vl3IV","tp":"line","d":[],"q":"The graph below shows the number of shops that closed and the number of new shops that opened in one country between 2011 and 2018. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Number of shop closures and openings, 2011–2018","x":["2011","2012","2013","2014","2015","2016","2017","2018"],"series":[{"n":"Closures","v":[6400,5900,7200,6500,600,5200,5000,5200]},{"n":"Openings","v":[8500,3900,5000,6200,4000,4000,4200,3000]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many shops closed and how many new shops opened each year in one country from 2011 to 2018.\n\nOverall, both closures and openings were lower at the end of the period than at the beginning, although neither followed a steady trend. Closures outnumbered openings in every year except 2011 and 2015.\n\nIn 2011, 8,500 new shops opened, well above the 6,400 that closed. Openings then more than halved to 3,900 in 2012, whereas closures fell only slightly before rising to a peak of 7,200 in 2013. By 2014 the two figures had almost converged, at 6,500 closures and 6,200 openings.\n\nThe most striking change came in 2015, when closures collapsed to just 600, while openings dropped to 4,000. This fall proved temporary: closures rebounded to 5,200 in 2016 and stayed at around 5,000 for the remaining years. Openings, by contrast, held steady at roughly 4,000 until 2017 and then declined to 3,000 in 2018, the lowest figure recorded and less than half the number seen at the start of the period.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 17 · Test 4"},
{"id":"d19TmDG","tp":"line","d":[],"q":"The graph below gives information about the percentage of the population in four Asian countries living in cities from 1970 to 2020, with predictions for 2030 and 2040. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Percentage of the population living in cities, 1970–2040","unit":"%","x":["1970","1980","1990","2000","2010","2020","2030","2040"],"series":[{"n":"Philippines","v":[32,34,48,45,42,45,50,56]},{"n":"Malaysia","v":[30,40,45,60,70,75,80,83]},{"n":"Thailand","v":[18,23,30,30,32,33,40,50]},{"n":"Indonesia","v":[14,17,25,30,43,52,60,64]}],"note":"Số liệu năm 2030 và 2040 là dự báo. Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the percentage of people living in cities in four Asian countries, the Philippines, Malaysia, Thailand and Indonesia, from 1970 to 2020, with predictions for 2030 and 2040.\n\nOverall, the urban share of the population grew in all four countries and is projected to keep growing. Malaysia became the most urbanised country, whereas Indonesia saw the fastest growth, rising from last place to second.\n\nIn 1970, about 30% of people in Malaysia and the Philippines lived in cities, roughly twice the proportion in Thailand (18%) and Indonesia (14%). Malaysia's figure then climbed rapidly to 75% in 2020 and is forecast to exceed 80% by 2040. The Philippines, by contrast, peaked at 48% in 1990, slipped back to 42% in 2010 and is predicted to recover to 56%.\n\nIndonesia's urban population share more than tripled to 52% in 2020, overtaking both Thailand and the Philippines, and is set to reach 64% by 2040. Thailand grew more slowly, staying at just over 30% between 1990 and 2020, although a sharper rise to 50% is anticipated in the final two decades.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 18 · Test 1"},
{"id":"d1u8Swy","tp":"line","d":[],"q":"The graph below shows the average monthly change in the prices of three metals during 2014. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Average monthly change in prices of copper, nickel and zinc, 2014 (% change compared with previous month)","unit":"% change","x":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"],"series":[{"n":"Copper","v":[2,1,0.5,0.5,-0.5,-0.5,1,1,1,0.5,0.5,1.5]},{"n":"Nickel","v":[6,4,1,1,0.5,-3,-1,-1,-1,-2,1,1]},{"n":"Zinc","v":[1,3,2,1.5,1,-1,-0.5,-0.5,-0.5,-1,1.5,2]}]},"mo":"The line graph shows the average monthly percentage change in the prices of three metals, copper, nickel and zinc, over the course of 2014.\n\nOverall, the prices of all three metals rose month on month at the start and end of the year, while nickel and zinc fell in price during the middle months. Nickel was by far the most volatile of the three, whereas copper showed the least variation.\n\nIn January, the price of nickel rose by 6%, three times the increase for copper (2%) and six times that for zinc (1%). The monthly rise for nickel then shrank rapidly to 1% in March, and in June its price actually fell by 3%, the largest drop on the graph. Nickel prices continued to fall by 1 to 2% a month until October, before rising by 1% in each of the last two months.\n\nZinc followed a similar but gentler pattern, with gains of up to 3% early in the year, small monthly losses from June to October and gains of 1.5 to 2% thereafter. Copper prices, by contrast, rose in ten of the twelve months, though never by more than 2%, and fell only slightly in May and June.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 18 · Test 4"},
{"id":"d1072VW","tp":"bar","d":["2023-07"],"q":"The charts below show the performance of a bus company in terms of punctuality, both actual and target, and the number of complaints made by passengers between 1999 and 2003. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Performance of a bus company, 1999–2003","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Percentage of services arriving on time (actual and target)","unit":"%","min":80,"x":["1999","2000","2001","2002","2003"],"series":[{"n":"Actual","v":[85,82,84.5,84,84.8]},{"n":"Target","v":[86,86,85,85,83.4]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Number of complaints (per thousand passenger journeys)","x":["1999","2000","2001","2002","2003"],"series":[{"n":"Complaints","v":[70,100,88,110,120]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề gốc. Trục dọc biểu đồ trên bắt đầu từ 80%. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The two bar charts show a bus company's performance between 1999 and 2003: the percentage of services that arrived on time compared with the company's target, and the number of complaints received per thousand passenger journeys.\n\nOverall, punctuality stayed below the target in every year except the last, when the target itself had been lowered. Over the same period, the number of complaints rose substantially.\n\nIn 1999, 85% of services arrived on time, just short of the 86% target. Punctuality fell to 82% the following year, four percentage points below target, which was the widest gap recorded. The target was then reduced to 85% for 2001 and 2002, when actual performance was 84.5% and 84% respectively. In 2003, the company finally exceeded its goal, although only after it had been cut to 83.4%, while actual punctuality was 84.8%.\n\nComplaints, by contrast, increased from 70 per thousand journeys in 1999 to 100 in 2000. After a brief improvement to 88 in 2001, the figure climbed again, reaching 120 in 2003, almost twice the original level.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1OhYVx","tp":"bar","d":["2023-07"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of male and female teachers in six different types of educational setting in the UK in 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Male and female teachers in six types of educational setting (%)","unit":"%","x":["Nursery / Pre-school","Primary school","Secondary school","College","Private training institute","University"],"series":[{"n":"Men","v":[5,10,44,50,57,70]},{"n":"Women","v":[95,90,56,50,43,30]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề gốc. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the proportions of male and female teachers in six types of educational institution, from nursery schools to universities, in the UK in 2010.\n\nOverall, women made up the great majority of teachers of young children, whereas men predominated at the highest levels of education. The gender balance was fairly even in secondary schools and colleges.\n\nIn nursery or pre-school settings, 95% of teachers were female and only 5% male, and the picture was similar in primary schools, where women made up 90% of staff. The gap was far narrower at secondary level, with women representing 56% of teachers and men 44%.\n\nColleges were the only institutions in which the two sexes were equally represented. Beyond that level, by contrast, men formed the majority: they accounted for 57% of teachers in private training institutes and 70% of those in universities, which was more than twice the proportion of women (30%). The share of male teachers was therefore fourteen times higher in universities than in nurseries.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1qE2zJ","tp":"bar","d":["2023-07"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of people in the UK eating more than five portions of vegetables and fish per day between 2011 and 2017. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"People in the UK eating more than 5 portions of vegetables and fish per day, 2011–2017 (%)","unit":"%","x":["2011","2012","2013","2014","2015","2016","2017"],"series":[{"n":"Women","v":[20,20,20,28,33,36,30]},{"n":"Children","v":[10,14,13,14,15,14,15]},{"n":"Men","v":[17.5,18,19,22,24,23,26]}],"note":"Tiêu đề chép theo hình trong bộ đề. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of women, men and children in the UK who ate more than five portions of vegetables and fish per day in each year from 2011 to 2017.\n\nOverall, women were the most likely to eat this amount throughout, and children the least likely. The figures for both women and men rose over the period, whereas the proportion of children changed little after an initial increase.\n\nBetween 2011 and 2013, exactly a fifth of women met this level of consumption. The figure then climbed rapidly, to 28% in 2014 and a peak of 36% in 2016, before falling back to 30% in the final year. The male figure, by contrast, grew more gradually and steadily, from about 17% in 2011 to 26% in 2017, when the gap between the sexes was only four percentage points.\n\nAmong children, 10% ate more than five portions a day in 2011. This rose to 14% the following year and then stayed between 13% and 15% for the rest of the period, which was roughly half the figure for women in most years.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1MrP1c","tp":"bar","d":["2023-05"],"q":"The charts compare the number of people per household in the UK in 1981 and 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Number of people per household in the UK, 1981 and 2001 (% of households)","unit":"%","x":["1-person","2-person","3-person","4-person","5-person","6-person"],"series":[{"n":"1981","v":[17,31,20,18,8,6]},{"n":"2001","v":[26,34,17,15,6,2]}]},"mo":"The charts compare the proportions of UK households of different sizes, from one person to six people, in 1981 and 2001.\n\nOverall, households became smaller over the twenty years. One- and two-person households made up a larger share of the total in 2001, whereas every category with three or more occupants became less common.\n\nIn 1981, two-person households were the most common type, at 31%, followed by three-person (20%) and four-person households (18%). People living alone accounted for 17%, while households of five and six people represented just 8% and 6% respectively.\n\nBy 2001, the share of single-person households had risen by nine percentage points to 26%, the largest change in any category, and two-person households had edged up to 34%. Together, these two types made up 60% of all households, compared with under half two decades earlier. Three- and four-person households, by contrast, had fallen to 17% and 15%, and the largest households had become rare, with six-person homes shrinking to a third of their former share, at 2%.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d13upRt","tp":"bar","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The chart below shows the expenditure on three categories among different age groups of residents in the UK in 2004. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Expenditure on three categories by age group, UK, 2004 (% of total spending)","unit":"% of total spending","x":["Under 30","31–45","46–60","61–75","76+"],"series":[{"n":"Food and drink","v":[6,13,17,20,23]},{"n":"Restaurant and hotel","v":[14,12,12,2,7]},{"n":"Entertainment","v":[7,8,14,23,13]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the share of total spending that UK residents in five age groups devoted to food and drink, restaurants and hotels, and entertainment in 2004.\n\nOverall, the proportion of spending that went on food and drink increased steadily with age, whereas spending on restaurants and hotels was highest among the young. Entertainment took up the largest share in late middle age and early retirement.\n\nPeople under 30 spent only 6% of their budget on food and drink, less than half the figure for restaurants and hotels (14%), which was by far their largest item of the three. The share for food and drink then rose in every successive group, doubling to 13% among those aged 31 to 45 and reaching 23% among people aged 76 and over, almost four times as much as the youngest group spent.\n\nSpending on restaurants and hotels stayed at around 12% up to the age of 60 but then fell to just 2% in the 61–75 group, before recovering to 7%. Entertainment, by contrast, peaked in that same group at 23%, having climbed from 7% among the youngest adults, and dropped back to 13% among the oldest residents.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1pEP0","tp":"bar","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The chart below gives information about the income and spending (in dollars) of Americans by age range in 2013. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Average income and expenditure of Americans by age range, 2013 ($)","unit":"$","x":["Under 25","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–64","65–74","Over 75"],"series":[{"n":"Income","v":[28000,59000,78000,79000,74000,53000,34000]},{"n":"Expenditure","v":[30000,48000,58000,60000,55000,47000,34000]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the average income and expenditure, in dollars, of Americans in seven age groups in 2013.\n\nOverall, both income and spending rose with age until middle age and then declined. Income exceeded expenditure in every group except the youngest and the oldest, and the surplus was largest among people between their mid-thirties and mid-sixties.\n\nAmericans under 25 earned about $28,000 on average but spent $30,000, making them the only group whose outgoings were higher than their income. Income then more than doubled to $59,000 in the next age group and peaked at just under $80,000 in the two groups covering the ages of 35 to 54. Spending also grew, although far less steeply, to a maximum of $60,000, which left a gap of roughly $20,000.\n\nAfter the age of 55, both figures fell. People aged 65 to 74 earned $53,000 and spent $47,000, so the difference had narrowed considerably, while among the over-75s income and expenditure were identical, at $34,000, only slightly above the levels recorded for the youngest group.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1aJaOm","tp":"bar","d":["2023-02"],"q":"The World Health Organization recommends that people should eat five or more portions of fruit and vegetables per day. The bar chart shows the percentage of males and females in the UK by age group who did so in 2006. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"People in the UK eating five or more portions of fruit and vegetables a day, by age, 2006 (%)","unit":"%","x":["15–25","26–40","41–55","56–65","66–74","75+"],"series":[{"n":"Males","v":[15,18,18,24,21,19]},{"n":"Females","v":[17,20,20,27,22,19]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of males and females in six age groups in the UK who ate the five or more daily portions of fruit and vegetables recommended by the World Health Organization in 2006.\n\nOverall, only a minority of people in any group met the recommendation. Women were slightly more likely than men to do so at almost every age, and the proportion was highest for both sexes among people in their late fifties and early sixties.\n\nIn the youngest group, aged 15 to 25, just 15% of males and 17% of females ate enough fruit and vegetables, the lowest figures on the chart. The proportions were somewhat higher, and identical, in the next two age groups, at 18% for males and 20% for females.\n\nConsumption peaked among 56-to-65-year-olds, of whom 24% of males and 27% of females met the target, a gap of three percentage points, which was the widest in any group. The figures then declined with age, to 21% and 22% respectively among those aged 66 to 74, while among people aged 75 and over the two sexes were level at 19%.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1LIkPP","tp":"bar","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The bar chart below shows shares of expenditures for five major categories in the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom, and Japan in the year 2009. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Shares of expenditure for five major categories, 2009 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Food","Housing","Transportation","Health care","Clothing"],"series":[{"n":"United States","v":[13,26,18,8,4]},{"n":"Canada","v":[15,21,20,4,6.5]},{"n":"United Kingdom","v":[21,24,15,2,6]},{"n":"Japan","v":[23,22,10,4,4.5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the shares of spending that went on five major categories, food, housing, transportation, health care and clothing, in the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom and Japan in 2009.\n\nOverall, housing, food and transportation absorbed the bulk of expenditure in all four countries, whereas health care and clothing each took less than a tenth. The two North American countries spent relatively more on transportation, while the UK and Japan devoted larger shares to food.\n\nHousing was the largest item in three of the four countries, ranging from 21% in Canada to 26% in the United States. Only in Japan was food the leading category, at 23%, slightly ahead of housing. Food also took 21% of spending in the UK, compared with just 15% in Canada and 13% in the US.\n\nThe pattern was reversed for transportation, which accounted for 20% of Canadian and 18% of American spending but only 10% in Japan, half the Canadian figure. Health care was notably more costly in the US, at 8%, twice the share in Canada and Japan and four times that in the UK. Spending on clothing was low everywhere, between 4% and 6.5%.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1PTT6O","tp":"bar","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The bar chart shows the percentage of households with cars in a European country between 1971 and 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Households with cars in a European country, 1971–2001 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1971","1981","1991","2001"],"series":[{"n":"No car","v":[47,42,30,28]},{"n":"1 car","v":[35,31,47,43]},{"n":"2 or more cars","v":[16,28,21,28]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of households in one European country that had no car, one car, or two or more cars in 1971, 1981, 1991 and 2001.\n\nOverall, car ownership became far more widespread over the thirty years. Far fewer households were without a car by the end, while the shares owning one car and several cars both finished higher than they had started.\n\nIn 1971, almost half of all households (47%) had no car, compared with 35% that had one and only 16% that had two or more. The car-free share declined to 42% in 1981 and then dropped sharply to 30% in 1991, before edging down to 28% in 2001.\n\nOne-car households followed a less regular path, dipping to 31% in 1981, jumping to a peak of 47% a decade later and then slipping to 43%, which nevertheless made them the largest group in the last two years shown. The figure for households with at least two cars, by contrast, rose to 28% in 1981, fell back to 21% in 1991 and returned to 28% in 2001, level with the proportion that had no car at all.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1x9jLA","tp":"bar","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The charts below show the percentage of people who were born in Australia and who were born outside Australia living in cities, towns and rural areas in 1995 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Where people born in and outside Australia lived, 1995 and 2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Born in Australia (1995)","Born outside Australia (1995)","Born in Australia (2010)","Born outside Australia (2010)"],"series":[{"n":"Cities","v":[50,60,65,80]},{"n":"Towns","v":[20,11,15,10]},{"n":"Rural areas","v":[30,40,15,7]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ hình trong bộ đề nên là số xấp xỉ (tổng mỗi nhóm có thể không tròn 100%)."},"mo":"The bar charts compare where people born in Australia and people born overseas lived, in cities, towns or rural areas, in 1995 and 2010.\n\nOverall, cities were home to the largest share of both groups in both years, and this share grew markedly over the period. Living in rural areas, by contrast, became far less common, particularly among those born outside Australia.\n\nIn 1995, half of all Australian-born residents lived in cities, compared with 60% of the overseas-born. Rural areas ranked second for both groups, at 30% and 40% respectively, while towns were the least common place of residence, accounting for a fifth of people born in Australia and only about a tenth of those born elsewhere.\n\nFifteen years later, the proportion of city dwellers had risen to 65% among the Australian-born and to 80% among immigrants. The rural figures, meanwhile, had fallen sharply, halving to 15% for the former group and dropping to just 7% for the latter, roughly a sixth of the earlier level. The share living in towns also declined slightly in both cases, to 15% and 10% respectively.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1xPjd3","tp":"bar","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The graph below shows the hours of teaching per year done by each teacher in four different countries in 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Hours of teaching per year per teacher in four countries, 2001","unit":"Hours","x":["Japan","Spain","Iceland","USA"],"series":[{"n":"Primary","v":[600,650,575,750]},{"n":"Lower secondary","v":[650,750,600,1000]},{"n":"Upper secondary","v":[700,900,925,1175]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the number of hours that primary, lower secondary and upper secondary teachers spent teaching per year in Japan, Spain, Iceland and the USA in 2001.\n\nOverall, teachers in the USA taught for the most hours at every level, while those in Japan generally taught the least. In all four countries, teaching hours increased with the level of education, although the size of the increase differed.\n\nAmerican primary teachers taught for 750 hours a year, compared with 575 to 650 hours in the other three countries. The gap was wider at lower secondary level, where the US figure of 1,000 hours was a third higher than Spain's (750) and far above those of Japan and Iceland, at 650 and 600 respectively. Upper secondary teachers in the USA worked the longest of all, at nearly 1,200 hours.\n\nJapan showed the least variation, with only 100 hours separating its primary and upper secondary teachers. In Iceland, by contrast, upper secondary staff taught for 925 hours, over 300 more than their lower secondary colleagues and slightly more than their Spanish counterparts (900).","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1rIy69","tp":"bar","d":["2022-12"],"q":"The chart below gives information about birth and death rates in Switzerland from 1970 to 2020 according to UN statistics. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Switzerland: births and deaths, 1970–2020 (millions)","unit":"Millions","x":["1970","1980","1990","2000","2010","2020"],"series":[{"n":"Births","v":[1.2,1.2,1.7,2.7,3.0,3.2]},{"n":"Deaths","v":[1.0,1.3,1.5,1.1,1.0,0.8]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột (theo hình trong bộ đề) nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the number of births and deaths in Switzerland, in millions, at ten-year intervals between 1970 and 2020, according to UN statistics.\n\nOverall, births rose substantially over the period, whereas deaths ended slightly lower than they had started. Consequently, the gap between the two, which was small until 1990, became very wide in the following decades.\n\nIn 1970, there were 1.2 million births and 1 million deaths. The number of births was unchanged ten years later, while deaths rose to 1.3 million, making 1980 the only year in which deaths exceeded births. By 1990, both figures had increased, to 1.7 million births and 1.5 million deaths.\n\nAfter 1990, the two trends diverged. Births jumped to 2.7 million in 2000 and continued to climb, passing 3 million in 2010 and ending at 3.2 million in 2020, more than two and a half times the 1970 level. Deaths, by contrast, fell back to 1.1 million in 2000 and then declined gradually to 0.8 million. Births therefore outnumbered deaths by four to one at the end of the period, compared with a difference of only 0.2 million at the beginning.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1oVp6A","tp":"bar","d":["2022-11"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentages of waste disposed of by four different methods in four cities (Toronto, Madrid, Kuala Lumpur and Amman). Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Waste disposal methods in four cities (% of total waste)","unit":"% of total waste","x":["Toronto","Madrid","Kuala Lumpur","Amman"],"series":[{"n":"Landfill","v":[75,29,15,48]},{"n":"Incineration","v":[11,40,55,38]},{"n":"Recycling","v":[8,23,23,11]},{"n":"Composting","v":[7,9,7,3]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the percentages of waste disposed of by four methods, landfill, incineration, recycling and composting, in Toronto, Madrid, Kuala Lumpur and Amman.\n\nOverall, landfill and incineration are the dominant methods in all four cities, although their relative importance varies widely. Recycling and composting together handle a minority of waste everywhere, and composting ranks last in each city.\n\nToronto relies overwhelmingly on landfill, which takes 75% of its waste, while the other three methods each handle roughly a tenth or less. Landfill is also the leading method in Amman, at 48%, although incineration is not far behind there, at 38%.\n\nIn Madrid and Kuala Lumpur, by contrast, incineration comes first. It is used for 55% of waste in Kuala Lumpur, more than three times the share sent to landfill (15%), and for 40% in Madrid, where landfill takes a further 29%. These two cities also recycle the most, at 23% each, roughly twice the proportion in Amman and almost three times that in Toronto. Composting is used for under 10% of waste in every city and for only 3% in Amman.","lv":"ex","rc":1,"pn":["Toronto","Kuala Lumpur","Amman","Madrid"]},
{"id":"d1agP","tp":"bar","d":["2022-06"],"q":"The charts below show what UK graduate and postgraduate students who did not go into full-time work did after leaving college in 2008. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Destinations of UK graduates and postgraduates (excluding full-time work), 2008","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Graduates","x":["Part-time work","Voluntary work","Further study","Unemployment"],"series":[{"n":"Number of graduates","v":[17735,3500,29665,16235]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Postgraduates","x":["Part-time work","Voluntary work","Further study","Unemployment"],"series":[{"n":"Number of postgraduates","v":[2535,345,2725,1625]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề gốc."},"mo":"The two bar charts show what UK graduates and postgraduates who did not enter full-time work did after completing their courses in 2008.\n\nOverall, further study was the most common destination for both groups, and voluntary work the least common. The main difference was that part-time work was almost as popular as further study among postgraduates, whereas it lagged far behind among graduates.\n\nOf the graduates, 29,665 went on to further study, making this by far the largest group. Part-time work and unemployment accounted for similar numbers, at 17,735 and 16,235 respectively, while only 3,500 took up voluntary work, less than an eighth of the figure for further study.\n\nThe postgraduate figures were roughly ten times smaller, but their ranking was the same. Further study attracted 2,725 postgraduates, only slightly more than the 2,535 who worked part-time, with a further 1,625 unemployed. Just 345 postgraduates did voluntary work, about a tenth of the graduate figure.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1yjlZI","tp":"bar","d":["2022-05"],"q":"The charts below show the percentage of internet users who do three online activities, both overall and by age group. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Online activities of internet users, overall and by age group (%)","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"% of all internet users","unit":"%","x":["Social networking","Paying bills online or online banking","Emailing"],"series":[{"n":"","v":[60,63,95]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"% of internet users in each age group","unit":"%","x":["15–24","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–64","65 or over"],"series":[{"n":"Social networking","v":[95,83,62,42,35,22]},{"n":"Paying bills online or online banking","v":[36,62,58,52,50,40]},{"n":"Emailing","v":[85,88,87,85,87,89]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The charts show the percentage of internet users who use social networking sites, pay bills or bank online and send emails, both overall and by age group.\n\nOverall, emailing is by far the most widespread of the three activities and is equally common at all ages. Social networking, by contrast, is strongly associated with younger users, while online banking is most popular among people in early middle age.\n\nTaking all users together, 95% send emails, compared with just over 60% who bank or pay bills online and a similar proportion who use social networks. Email use varies very little with age, ranging only from 85% to 89% across the six groups.\n\nThe pattern for social networking is very different. It is used by 95% of 15-to-24-year-olds, but the figure is lower in each successive age group, at 62% among those aged 35 to 44 and only 22% among users aged 65 or over. Online banking is least common among the youngest users, at 36%, is at its highest (62%) in the 25–34 group and then becomes gradually less common, with 40% of the oldest group using it.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1HRBH","tp":"bar","d":["2022-04"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of the population in the UK who consumed the recommended daily amount of fruit and vegetables in 2002, 2006 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"People in the UK who consumed the recommended daily amount of fruit and vegetables (%)","unit":"%","x":["2002","2006","2010"],"series":[{"n":"Men","v":[22,28,24]},{"n":"Women","v":[25,32,27]},{"n":"Children","v":[11,16,14]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of men, women and children in the UK who ate the recommended daily amount of fruit and vegetables in 2002, 2006 and 2010.\n\nOverall, women were the most likely to meet the recommendation in all three years, while children were by far the least likely. For every group, the figure rose between 2002 and 2006 and then fell back, although it ended above its starting level.\n\nIn 2002, a quarter of women consumed enough fruit and vegetables, compared with 22% of men and only 11% of children, less than half the female figure. Four years later, all three proportions had increased markedly: women reached 32%, the highest figure on the chart, men 28% and children 16%.\n\nBy 2010, however, each figure had declined, with the share of women meeting the target dropping by five percentage points to 27%. That of men fell to 24%, while the proportion of children slipped only slightly, to 14%. Consequently, the gap between adults and children, which had been widest in 2006, narrowed a little by the end of the period.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1k9QC","tp":"bar","d":["2022-02"],"q":"The bar chart shows the percentage of school children learning to play four different musical instruments (violin, guitar, piano, drums) in 2005, 2010 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"School children learning to play four musical instruments, 2005, 2010 and 2015 (%)","unit":"%","x":["2005","2010","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Guitar","v":[11,20,27]},{"n":"Piano","v":[10,17,25]},{"n":"Drums","v":[6,6,8]},{"n":"Violin","v":[8,8,7]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of school children who were learning to play four musical instruments, the guitar, piano, drums and violin, in 2005, 2010 and 2015.\n\nOverall, the guitar and the piano were the most popular instruments in every year, and both attracted a rapidly growing share of pupils. By contrast, the proportions learning the drums and the violin stayed low and changed very little.\n\nIn 2005, 11% of children were learning the guitar and 10% the piano, only slightly more than the 8% who played the violin and the 6% who played the drums. Five years later, the guitar figure had almost doubled to 20%, while the piano had risen to 17%. Both continued to climb, ending at 27% and 25% respectively in 2015, roughly two and a half times their original levels.\n\nThe other two instruments showed no such growth. The share of drum learners was still 6% in 2010 and rose only to 8% in 2015, whereas the violin stayed at 8% before slipping to 7%, which made it the least popular instrument at the end of the period.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1KbpA8","tp":"bar","d":["2022-01"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentages of people using three types of vehicle (car, motorcycle and bicycle) in four countries. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"People using three types of vehicle in four countries (%)","unit":"%","x":["Great Britain","USA","Germany","France"],"series":[{"n":"Car","v":[60,60,40,75]},{"n":"Motorcycle","v":[10,30,15,10]},{"n":"Bicycle","v":[29,10,45,15]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình và đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the percentages of people using cars, motorcycles and bicycles in four countries, Great Britain, the USA, Germany and France.\n\nOverall, the car is more widely used than the other two vehicles in every country except Germany, where the bicycle is slightly more popular. Motorcycles are the least common choice in three of the four countries.\n\nCar use is highest in France, at 75%, five times the figure for bicycles there and far above that for motorcycles (10%). In Great Britain and the USA, 60% of people use a car. These two countries differ, however, in their second choice: 29% of Britons cycle, compared with only 10% who ride a motorcycle, whereas in the USA the proportions are reversed, with 30% using a motorcycle and 10% a bicycle.\n\nGermany shows a different pattern. Only 40% of people there use a car, the lowest figure of the four countries, while 45% travel by bicycle, three times the proportion of motorcyclists (15%). The German cycling rate is therefore well above that of Britain and more than four times that of the USA.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1MCcIA","tp":"bar","d":["2022-01"],"q":"The chart below shows the number of people employed in five types of work in one region of Australia in 2001 and 2008. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Number of people employed in five types of work, 2001 and 2008","x":["Sales","Accounting","Computing","Nursing","Farming"],"series":[{"n":"2001","v":[154000,60000,52000,52000,22000]},{"n":"2008","v":[166000,58000,65000,58000,19000]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the number of people employed in five types of work in one region of Australia in 2001 and 2008.\n\nOverall, sales employed far more people than any other sector in both years, while farming employed the fewest. Employment rose in sales, computing and nursing, whereas it declined slightly in accounting and farming.\n\nIn 2001, about 154,000 people worked in sales, more than twice as many as in accounting (60,000), the second-largest sector. By 2008, the sales workforce had grown to 166,000, widening the gap further, while the number of accountants had slipped to 58,000.\n\nComputing and nursing each employed 52,000 people in 2001. Computing then recorded the strongest growth of any sector, rising by a quarter to 65,000 and overtaking accounting, whereas nursing increased more modestly to 58,000, the same figure as accounting. Farming was by far the smallest of the five, and its workforce shrank from 22,000 to 19,000, roughly a ninth of the number working in sales.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d13UPlP","tp":"bar","d":["2021-12"],"q":"The graph below gives information about the common activities children of different ages in the United States do as part of their bedtime routines. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Common activities for children as part of bedtime routine (% of households)","unit":"% of households","x":["Reading","Brushing teeth","Having a bath","Watching TV","Eating"],"series":[{"n":"1–3 years","v":[65,30,40,16,20]},{"n":"4–5 years","v":[70,45,35,20,10]},{"n":"6–8 years","v":[58,50,32,18,8]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of American households in which children in three age groups do five activities as part of their bedtime routine.\n\nOverall, reading is by far the most common bedtime activity for all three age groups, while watching television and eating are the least common. Brushing teeth becomes more usual as children get older, whereas having a bath and eating become less so.\n\nReading forms part of the routine in 65% of households with children aged 1 to 3, 70% of those with 4-to-5-year-olds and just under 60% of those with children aged 6 to 8. Brushing teeth shows the clearest upward pattern, climbing from 30% for the youngest group to 50% for the oldest, which makes it the second most common activity for school-age children.\n\nHaving a bath, by contrast, is most typical of the youngest children, at 40%, and is down to roughly 32% among the oldest. Eating before bed follows the same pattern, halving from 20% to 10% between the first two age groups. Television is watched in fewer than a fifth of households at any age.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1HBBEd","tp":"bar","d":["2021-12"],"q":"The graph below shows information on employment rates across six countries in 1995 and 2005. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Employment rates across six countries, 1995 and 2005 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Australia","Switzerland","Iceland","UK","New Zealand","USA"],"series":[{"n":"Male, 1995","v":[57,65,72,55,60,59]},{"n":"Male, 2005","v":[69,78,82,73,72,72]},{"n":"Female, 1995","v":[28,55,40,52,25,45]},{"n":"Female, 2005","v":[38,68,52,62,43,62]}],"note":"Trên hình đề thiếu tên nước thứ sáu; theo đề gốc là USA. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the employment rates of men and women in six countries, Australia, Switzerland, Iceland, the UK, New Zealand and the USA, in 1995 and 2005.\n\nOverall, employment rose for both sexes in all six countries over the decade. Men were more likely to be in work than women everywhere and in both years, although the size of this gap varied considerably from country to country.\n\nAmong men, Iceland had the highest rate in both years, at 72% in 1995 and 82% in 2005, followed by Switzerland, where the figure climbed from 65% to 78%. The other four countries started between 55% and 60% and all ended at roughly 70% or slightly more, with the UK recording the largest gain, of 18 percentage points.\n\nFemale employment was lowest in New Zealand and Australia in 1995, at just 25% and 28% respectively, less than half the male rates there. By contrast, more than half of women in Switzerland and the UK were already working. Ten years later, New Zealand's figure had jumped to 43%, while Switzerland still led with 68%, well ahead of the UK and the USA, both at 62%.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1rDUDx","tp":"bar","d":["2021-12"],"q":"The bar chart gives information about the railway system in six cities in Europe. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Railway systems in six European cities: passengers per year (millions) and year of opening","unit":"Million passengers","x":["Paris (opened 1863)","Stockholm (1900)","Lisbon (1927)","Rome (1976)","Madrid (1981)","Berlin (2001)"],"series":[{"n":"Passengers per year (millions)","v":[775,1191,1927,144,45,50]}],"note":"Số liệu và năm khai trương chép theo hình trong bộ đề."},"mo":"The bar chart shows how many passengers use the railway systems of six European cities each year, together with the year in which each system opened.\n\nOverall, the three oldest systems carry vastly more passengers than the three newest ones. Lisbon's railway is by far the busiest, whereas those in Madrid and Berlin are used the least.\n\nThe railways in Paris, Stockholm and Lisbon all opened before 1930. Paris has the oldest system, dating from 1863, and it carries 775 million passengers a year. Stockholm's network, which opened in 1900, is used by 1,191 million people annually, while Lisbon's, opened in 1927, handles 1,927 million, roughly two and a half times the Paris figure.\n\nBy contrast, the three systems built in the last quarter of the twentieth century or later are far smaller in terms of traffic. Rome's railway, opened in 1976, carries 144 million passengers a year, less than a tenth of the Lisbon total. The figures for Madrid (opened in 1981) and Berlin (2001) are lower still, at 45 million and 50 million respectively, giving a combined total of under 100 million.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1remXn","tp":"bar","d":["2021-10"],"q":"The bar chart below provides information about the percentages of students who are proficient in a foreign language in different countries. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Students proficient in a foreign language, by country and gender (%)","unit":"%","x":["China","Romania","Thailand","India","Russia","Vietnam"],"series":[{"n":"Males","v":[17,42,32,56,35,39]},{"n":"Females","v":[32,65,27,68,43,56]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of male and female students who are proficient in a foreign language in six countries, China, Romania, Thailand, India, Russia and Vietnam.\n\nOverall, female students are more likely than male students to be proficient in every country except Thailand. India has the highest proficiency rates for both sexes, whereas China and Thailand have the lowest.\n\nIn India, 68% of female students and 56% of male students have mastered a foreign language. Romania comes second for girls, at 65%, although the figure for boys there is considerably lower, at 42%, which is the widest gender gap of the six countries. Vietnam shows a similar pattern, with 56% of females proficient compared with 39% of males.\n\nThe differences are smaller elsewhere. In Russia, 43% of girls and 35% of boys reach proficiency, while in China the corresponding figures are 32% and just 17%, the lowest male rate on the chart. Thailand is the only country where boys outperform girls, by 32% to 27%, the latter being the lowest figure for female students.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1xXU32","tp":"bar","d":["2021-08"],"q":"The chart below gives information on the percentage of British people giving money to charity by age range for the years 1990 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"British people giving money to charity, by age, 1990 and 2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["18–25","26–35","36–50","51–65","Over 65"],"series":[{"n":"1990","v":[17,30,41,35,31]},{"n":"2010","v":[7,24,35,39,34]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the proportion of British people in five age groups who gave money to charity in 1990 and 2010.\n\nOverall, people in the three younger age groups were less likely to donate in 2010 than in 1990, whereas the two oldest groups became more generous. Consequently, the group with the highest proportion of donors was no longer the middle-aged one but the next oldest.\n\nIn 1990, giving was most common among people aged 36 to 50, 41% of whom donated, followed by the group immediately above them (35%). The youngest adults were the least likely to give, at 17%. Two decades later, the youngest group's figure had more than halved to 7%, while the two groups in the middle had each fallen by six percentage points, to 24% and 35% respectively.\n\nBy contrast, the share of donors among people aged 51 to 65 rose to 39%, the highest figure in 2010, and that for the over-65s climbed from 31% to 34%. The gap between the youngest and oldest groups therefore widened considerably, from 14 to 27 percentage points.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1AH43","tp":"bar","d":["2021-07"],"q":"The graph below shows the percentage of people going to cinemas in one European country on different days. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"People going to cinemas in one European country on different days, 2003, 2005 and 2007 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Mon","Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri","Sat","Sun"],"series":[{"n":"2003","v":[10,20,16,12,30,40,30]},{"n":"2005","v":[14,17,14,9,30,45,34]},{"n":"2007","v":[12,19,9,14,30,43,32]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of people in one European country who went to the cinema on each day of the week in 2003, 2005 and 2007.\n\nOverall, cinemas were far busier from Friday to Sunday than on weekdays, with Saturday the most popular day in all three years. Attendance on most days changed only slightly over the period.\n\nOn Saturdays, 40% of people went to the cinema in 2003, a figure that rose to 45% in 2005 before easing to 43%. Sunday followed a similar pattern at a lower level, moving from 30% to 34% and then 32%, while the Friday figure was exactly 30% in every year.\n\nAttendance from Monday to Thursday was much lower, never exceeding 20%. Tuesday was the most popular of these days, at between 17% and 20%, roughly half the Saturday figure. Monday and Thursday were generally the quietest, each attracting 9 to 14% of people, whereas Wednesday showed the clearest decline, falling from 16% in 2003 to just 9% in 2007, the joint-lowest figure on the chart.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1qvcvs","tp":"bar","d":["2021-05"],"q":"The bar chart shows the number of people who visited different museums in London. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Visitors to four museums in London, June–October","x":["June","July","August","September","October"],"series":[{"n":"History Museum","v":[400000,400000,600000,380000,250000]},{"n":"British Museum","v":[420000,420000,720000,590000,450000]},{"n":"National Museum","v":[215000,185000,320000,200000,180000]},{"n":"Science Museum","v":[440000,400000,400000,500000,300000]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows how many people visited four museums in London, the History Museum, the British Museum, the National Museum and the Science Museum, in each month from June to October.\n\nOverall, the British Museum attracted the most visitors in four of the five months, while the National Museum was consistently the least visited. Attendance at three of the museums peaked in August, and all four received fewer visitors in October than in June.\n\nIn June and July, the History, British and Science Museums each welcomed roughly 400,000 visitors a month, about twice as many as the National Museum. August brought a sharp increase: the British Museum received 720,000 visitors and the History Museum 600,000, while the National Museum recorded its highest figure, at 320,000. The Science Museum, by contrast, stayed at 400,000.\n\nAfter August, numbers generally fell. The British Museum dropped to 590,000 in September and 450,000 in October, although it was still the most popular. The Science Museum peaked a month later than the others, at 500,000 in September, before falling to 300,000. The History Museum saw the steepest decline, ending at 250,000, less than half its August figure.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d17F5KK","tp":"bar","d":["2021-04"],"q":"The bar chart below shows the passenger kilometres travelled by different means of transport in the UK in 1990 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Passenger kilometres by different means of transport, UK, 1990 and 2000 (billions)","unit":"Billion km","x":["Bicycle","Motorbike","Air","Bus","Rail","Total"],"series":[{"n":"1990","v":[5,6,5,44,40,100]},{"n":"2000","v":[4,5,8,47,47,112]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the number of passenger kilometres travelled by five means of transport in the UK in 1990 and 2000, measured in billions.\n\nOverall, total passenger travel increased over the decade. Buses and trains carried the vast majority of traffic in both years, while bicycles, motorbikes and aircraft each made only a small contribution, with air travel the only one of the three to grow.\n\nIn 1990, buses were the most heavily used mode, at 44 billion passenger kilometres, followed by rail at 40 billion. Both figures had risen by 2000, although rail grew faster, climbing by 7 billion to draw level with buses at 47 billion. Together, these two modes were responsible for well over four-fifths of the total, which went up from 100 billion to 112 billion.\n\nThe remaining modes each accounted for fewer than 10 billion kilometres. Travel by bicycle and motorbike declined slightly, from 5 to 4 billion and from 6 to 5 billion respectively, whereas air travel rose from 5 to 8 billion, an increase of more than half, overtaking both of the others.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1I2On","tp":"bar","d":["2021-04"],"q":"The bar chart shows the types of media used to get daily news, by age group, in 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Types of media used to get daily news, by age group, 2011 (%)","unit":"%","x":["10–17","18–29","30–49","50–64","65+"],"series":[{"n":"Social networking","v":[80,80,50,40,25]},{"n":"Microblogging","v":[20,25,18,15,10]},{"n":"Radio","v":[40,92,90,88,85]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of people in five age groups who used social networking sites, microblogging services and radio to get daily news in 2011.\n\nOverall, radio was the main source of daily news for every age group except the youngest, whereas microblogging was the least used medium at all ages. Reliance on social networking declined steadily with age.\n\nAmong 10-to-17-year-olds, social networking was by far the most popular source, used by 80%, twice the proportion who listened to the radio. The same share of 18-to-29-year-olds used social networks, but the figure then dropped to half of those aged 30 to 49, 40% of the 50–64 group and only a quarter of people aged 65 or over.\n\nRadio showed almost the opposite pattern. Having been used by just 40% of the youngest group, it was the choice of over 90% of young adults and stayed above 85% in all the older groups. Microblogging, by contrast, never exceeded 25%, peaking among young adults and falling to 10% among the oldest respondents, less than an eighth of the figure for radio.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1bLjxW","tp":"bar","d":["2021-03"],"q":"The charts below give information about the amount of time children spend with their parents on weekdays and at weekends. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Time children spend with their parents, by age (hours per day)","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Weekdays","unit":"Hours","x":["Age 0–1","Age 2–3","Age 4–5","Age 6–7","Age 8–9"],"series":[{"n":"With Dad","v":[2,1,2.2,1,1.9]},{"n":"With Mom","v":[5.5,5.9,5.1,5.5,5]},{"n":"Both Dad & Mom","v":[1.5,1.8,1.4,2.4,2.4]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Weekends","unit":"Hours","x":["Age 0–1","Age 2–3","Age 4–5","Age 6–7","Age 8–9"],"series":[{"n":"With Dad","v":[0.5,1,1.5,0.6,0.3]},{"n":"With Mom","v":[3.5,3.2,3,3.9,3.7]},{"n":"Both Dad & Mom","v":[4.9,5.9,5.8,5.6,5.4]}]}],"note":"Hình gốc là biểu đồ cột chồng; ở đây vẽ thành cột ghép. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The charts show how many hours a day children of different ages, from birth to nine, spend with their father, with their mother and with both parents together on weekdays and at weekends.\n\nOverall, children spend around nine to ten hours a day with their parents, slightly more at weekends than on weekdays. On weekdays most of this time is spent with the mother alone, whereas at weekends time with both parents together is the largest component.\n\nOn weekdays, children in every age group spend between 5 and 6 hours with their mother, far more than the 1 to 2 hours they spend with their father. Time with both parents is also limited, at about 1.5 hours for the youngest children and 2.4 hours for those aged six and over.\n\nAt weekends, by contrast, children spend roughly 5 to 6 hours with both parents, three or four times the weekday figure. Time with the mother alone is reduced to between 3 and 4 hours, although this is still several times the amount spent with the father, which is highest for 4-to-5-year-olds, at 1.5 hours, and under half an hour for the oldest children.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1hpQmB","tp":"bar","d":["2021-02"],"q":"The bar chart shows the percentage of small, medium and large companies which used social media for business purposes between 2012 and 2016. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Businesses that have a social media presence, 2012–2016 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Small business","Medium business","Large business"],"series":[{"n":"2012","v":[27,32,78]},{"n":"2013","v":[30,48,78]},{"n":"2014","v":[35,49,77]},{"n":"2015","v":[30,30,53]},{"n":"2016","v":[49,53,79]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of small, medium-sized and large businesses that used social media for business purposes each year from 2012 to 2016.\n\nOverall, large companies were far more likely to have a social media presence than smaller ones throughout the period. Usage rose in all three categories over the five years, although each saw a temporary fall in the fourth year.\n\nBetween 2012 and 2014, just under 80% of large businesses used social media, more than twice the proportion of small firms, whose figure climbed gradually from 27% to 35%. Medium-sized companies started only slightly ahead of small ones, at 32%, but jumped to almost half in 2013 and stayed at that level the following year.\n\nIn 2015, usage fell in every category. The decline was steepest among large businesses, whose figure dropped to 53%, while both small and medium-sized firms slipped to 30%. All three groups then recovered strongly in 2016, reaching their highest levels of the period: 79% for large companies, 53% for medium-sized ones and 49% for small ones, which meant that the gap between the largest and smallest firms had narrowed considerably.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d14niMU","tp":"bar","d":["2020-08"],"q":"The bar chart below shows the results of a survey conducted over a three-year period to discover what people who live in London think of the city. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"What are the best things about living in London? (% of respondents)","unit":"%","x":["Range of shops","Job opportunities","Mixture of people","Museums & art galleries","Night life","Transport"],"series":[{"n":"Year 1","v":[45,38,36,22,25,22]},{"n":"Year 2","v":[42,35,36,24,20,20]},{"n":"Year 3","v":[43,35,35,26,22,25]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the results of a survey, carried out in three consecutive years, in which London residents were asked what they considered the best things about living in the city.\n\nOverall, the range of shops was the most frequently chosen advantage in all three years, followed by job opportunities and the mixture of people. Museums and galleries, night life and transport were each selected by far fewer respondents, and opinions changed only slightly from year to year.\n\nIn the first year, 45% of residents named the range of shops, a figure that dipped to 42% and then recovered to 43%. Job opportunities were mentioned by 38% at first and by 35% in the following two years, which was almost identical to the proportion choosing the mixture of people (35 to 36% throughout).\n\nThe other three features attracted between a fifth and a quarter of respondents. Support for museums and art galleries rose steadily, from 22% to 26%, whereas night life fell from 25% to 20% before recovering slightly. Transport was the least popular in the second year, at 20%, but climbed to 25% in the third, just below museums and galleries.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1X4dE","tp":"bar","d":["2020-03"],"q":"The bar chart shows the percentage of young people in higher education in four countries in 2000, 2005 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Young people in higher education in four countries, 2000, 2005 and 2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Country A","Country B","Country C","Country D"],"series":[{"n":"2000","v":[36,40,43,45]},{"n":"2005","v":[48,39,47,50]},{"n":"2010","v":[57,40,47,60]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of young people in higher education in four countries, labelled A to D, in 2000, 2005 and 2010.\n\nOverall, participation in higher education increased in three of the four countries, most strongly in Country A. Country D had the highest rate in every year, while Country B was the only one in which the figure did not rise.\n\nIn 2000, Country D led with 45% of young people in higher education, followed by Country C (43%), Country B (40%) and Country A (36%). Over the next ten years, Country A recorded the fastest growth, climbing by twelve percentage points to 48% in 2005 and reaching 57% in 2010, which moved it from last place to second.\n\nCountry D also rose steadily, to 50% in 2005 and 60% in 2010, the highest figure on the chart. Country C, by contrast, increased only slightly, to 47%, and then levelled off, whereas the proportion in Country B stayed at roughly 40% throughout. Consequently, the gap between the highest and lowest rates widened from nine to twenty percentage points.","lv":"in","pn":["Country"]},
{"id":"d1pF727","tp":"bar","d":["2020-02"],"q":"The bar chart below shows the amount of money invested in each category by five organisations. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Money invested in four categories by five organisations (billion euros)","unit":"Billion euros","x":["Organisation A","Organisation B","Organisation C","Organisation D","Organisation E"],"series":[{"n":"Machinery","v":[1.0,0.9,1.3,0.9,0.8]},{"n":"Building","v":[1.5,1.4,1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"n":"Staff training","v":[1.2,1.9,0.8,0.7,0.9]},{"n":"Research","v":[1.1,1.6,1.3,0.6,0.7]}]},"mo":"The bar chart shows how much money five organisations, labelled A to E, invested in machinery, building, staff training and research, measured in billions of euros.\n\nOverall, Organisation B invested the most in total and Organisations D and E the least. Spending priorities varied considerably: building was the largest or joint-largest item for three of the organisations, whereas B concentrated on staff training and C on machinery and research.\n\nOrganisation B spent 1.9 billion euros on staff training, the highest single figure on the chart, together with 1.6 billion on research and 1.4 billion on building, but only 0.9 billion on machinery. Organisation A, by contrast, put most of its money into building (1.5 billion), with between 1 and 1.2 billion going to each of the other three areas.\n\nOrganisation C invested 1.3 billion in both machinery and research, compared with 1 billion in building and just 0.8 billion in staff training. The two smallest investors, D and E, each spent 1 billion on building, while none of their other investments exceeded 0.9 billion. Research received the least in both cases, at 0.6 and 0.7 billion respectively.","lv":"in","pn":["Organisation"]},
{"id":"d1CiMv9","tp":"bar","d":["2019-08"],"q":"The chart below shows water levels in reservoirs in six cities in Australia in October 2009 and October 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Water levels in reservoirs in six Australian cities, October 2009 and October 2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Perth","Canberra","Melbourne","Darwin","Sydney","Brisbane"],"series":[{"n":"Oct 2009","v":[47,30,70,50,47,70]},{"n":"Oct 2010","v":[37,40,80,70,47,90]}],"note":"Hình đề không ghi đơn vị; thang 0–100 được hiểu là phần trăm dung tích hồ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the water levels in the reservoirs of six Australian cities in October 2009 and October 2010.\n\nOverall, water levels rose in four of the six cities over the year, with Brisbane and Darwin recording the largest gains. Perth was the only city whose reservoir level fell, while Sydney's did not change.\n\nIn October 2009, the reservoirs of Melbourne and Brisbane were the fullest, both standing at 70%, compared with 50% in Darwin and just under that figure in Perth and Sydney. Canberra had by far the lowest level, at only 30%.\n\nA year later, Brisbane's level had climbed by 20 percentage points to 90%, the highest figure on the chart, and Darwin's had risen by the same amount to 70%. Melbourne and Canberra each gained 10 points, ending at 80% and 40% respectively. By contrast, the level in Perth dropped from 47% to 37%, which left it with the least water of the six cities, whereas Sydney was unchanged at 47%.","lv":"in","pn":["Melbourne","Brisbane","Darwin","Perth","Canberra"]},
{"id":"d1SdVo","tp":"bar","d":["2019-06"],"q":"The chart below shows the number of jobs in tourism-related industries in one UK city between 1989 and 2009. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Jobs in tourism-related industries in one UK city, 1989–2009","unit":"Jobs","x":["1989","1994","1999","2004","2009"],"series":[{"n":"Restaurants","v":[1100,1280,1430,1600,1340]},{"n":"Hotels","v":[1060,1400,1250,1160,1040]},{"n":"Sport and leisure","v":[900,670,1060,870,700]},{"n":"Travel and tours","v":[800,1050,870,670,440]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the number of jobs in four tourism-related industries, restaurants, hotels, sport and leisure, and travel and tours, in one UK city between 1989 and 2009.\n\nOverall, restaurants were the largest employer in most years and the only sector with more jobs at the end of the period than at the beginning. Employment in travel and tours fell the most, almost halving over the twenty years.\n\nIn 1989, restaurants and hotels each provided roughly 1,100 jobs. Restaurant employment then grew steadily to a peak of 1,600 in 2004, before falling back to about 1,340. Hotels, by contrast, peaked much earlier, at 1,400 jobs in 1994, when they briefly led the four sectors, and then declined in every subsequent period, ending slightly below their starting level.\n\nThe other two industries fluctuated. Sport and leisure dropped from 900 jobs to 670 in 1994, recovered to 1,060 five years later and finished at 700, whereas travel and tours moved in the opposite direction at first, rising from 800 to 1,050, but then shrank continuously to 440 in 2009, the lowest figure on the chart.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1yd2bK","tp":"bar","d":["2019-06"],"q":"The chart shows the number of students in a UK university who completed (on time or late), failed to write, and rewrote their dissertation in 1990, 2000 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Students at a UK university: dissertation outcomes, 1990, 2000 and 2010","unit":"Students","x":["Completed (on time)","Completed (late)","Failed to write","Rewrote"],"series":[{"n":"1990","v":[150,18,42,10]},{"n":"2000","v":[205,27,23,26]},{"n":"2010","v":[300,43,8,23]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the number of students at a UK university who completed their dissertation on time, completed it late, failed to write it or had to rewrite it in 1990, 2000 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the vast majority of students finished their dissertations on time in all three years, and their number doubled over the period. The number who failed to write a dissertation fell steadily, whereas late completions rose.\n\nIn 1990, 150 students completed on time, a figure that increased to just over 200 in 2000 and 300 in 2010, which was seven times the size of the next largest group. Late completions also grew, although from a much lower base, climbing from 18 to 27 and then to 43.\n\nBy contrast, the number of students who failed to write a dissertation declined sharply, from 42 in 1990 to 23 a decade later and only 8 in 2010, less than a fifth of the original figure. Those who rewrote their work numbered just 10 in 1990, rose to 26 in 2000 and then dipped slightly to 23.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1q3sev","tp":"bar","d":["2019-05"],"q":"The bar chart shows the number of cars per 1,000 people in five countries in Europe in three years, 2005, 2009 and 2015, compared with the European average. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Cars per 1,000 people in five European countries, 2005, 2009 and 2015","unit":"Cars per 1,000 people","x":["Country 1","Country 2","Country 3","Country 4","Country 5","Average of 5"],"series":[{"n":"2005","v":[310,500,650,700,920,700]},{"n":"2009","v":[400,550,600,700,880,680]},{"n":"2015","v":[310,530,560,700,970,780]}],"note":"Hình đề không có chú giải năm; ba cột được hiểu theo thứ tự 2005, 2009, 2015. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the number of cars per thousand people in five European countries in 2005, 2009 and 2015, together with the average for the five.\n\nOverall, car ownership differed widely between the countries, with the fifth country having roughly three times as many cars per head as the first. Ownership changed relatively little in most countries, although the average was higher at the end of the period than at the beginning.\n\nThe fifth country had the highest level throughout, at about 920 cars per thousand people in 2005, a slight dip to 880 in 2009 and a peak of 970 in 2015. The fourth country was the most stable, recording exactly 700 in all three years, which matched the five-country average in 2005. The average itself slipped slightly to 680 before rising to 780.\n\nThe other three countries were below average in every year. The third saw a steady decline, from 650 to 560, whereas the second fluctuated between 500 and 550. The first country had the lowest level of ownership, starting and ending at around 310 per thousand people, despite a temporary increase to 400 in 2009.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d11FIv6","tp":"bar","d":["2019-04"],"q":"The chart below shows information about fuel used in the transport sector in different countries in Europe, compared to the EU average, in 2009 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Percentage of fuel used in the transport sector in six EU countries, compared with the average, 2009 and 2010","unit":"%","x":["Slovakia","Italy","France","Austria","Spain","Greece","EU average"],"series":[{"n":"2009","v":[8.8,4.5,3.7,5.2,3.8,1.2,6]},{"n":"2010","v":[6.5,5.2,4.3,5.2,3,2,5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of fuel used in the transport sector in six European countries in 2009 and 2010, compared with the EU average.\n\nOverall, Slovakia had by far the highest figure in both years, while Greece had the lowest. The percentage fell in Slovakia and Spain, as did the EU average, whereas it rose in Italy, France and Greece.\n\nIn 2009, Slovakia's figure stood at 8.8%, well above the EU average of 6% and more than seven times the Greek figure of 1.2%. Although it dropped to 6.5% the following year, Slovakia was still the only country above the EU average, which had itself declined to 5%.\n\nAustria recorded 5.2% in both years, and Italy rose from 4.5% to the same level in 2010, so that both slightly exceeded the average that year. France also saw an increase, from 3.7% to 4.3%, while Spain moved in the opposite direction, falling from 3.8% to 3%. Greece's share almost doubled to 2%, although it was still the smallest of the six.","lv":"in","pn":["Slovakia"]},
{"id":"d1Tg3X","tp":"bar","d":["2019-04"],"q":"The chart gives information about the percentage of overweight men and women in Australia from 1980 to 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Percentage of overweight men and women, Australia, 1980–2010","unit":"%","x":["Male","Female"],"series":[{"n":"1980","v":[47,30]},{"n":"1990","v":[51,40]},{"n":"2000","v":[70,50]},{"n":"2010","v":[68,50]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of men and women in Australia who were overweight in 1980, 1990, 2000 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the proportion of overweight people rose substantially for both sexes over the thirty years, although it levelled off in the final decade. Men were more likely than women to be overweight in every year shown.\n\nIn 1980, 47% of Australian men were overweight, compared with 30% of women. The male figure edged up to 51% in 1990 and then jumped to 70% in 2000, the highest level recorded, before easing slightly to 68% in 2010.\n\nThe female figure rose more evenly at first, climbing by ten percentage points in each of the first two decades, from 30% to 40% and then to exactly half in 2000, after which it did not change. The gap between the sexes was therefore narrowest in 1990, at 11 percentage points, and widest in 2000, when it stood at 20 points. By the end of the period, roughly two in three men and one in two women were overweight.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1WYEKw","tp":"bar","d":["2019-02"],"q":"The bar chart shows the average class size in primary schools and lower secondary schools in six countries compared to the world average in 2006. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Average number of pupils per class in primary and lower secondary schools, 2006","unit":"Pupils","x":["South Korea","Japan","UK","Mexico","Denmark","Iceland","World"],"series":[{"n":"Primary","v":[34,29,27,20,23,21,25]},{"n":"Lower secondary","v":[36,34,18,30,21,20,28]}]},"mo":"The bar chart compares the average number of pupils per class in primary and lower secondary schools in six countries with the world average in 2006.\n\nOverall, classes were largest in the two Asian countries and smallest in the two Nordic ones. In most cases lower secondary classes were bigger than primary classes, although the UK was a striking exception.\n\nSouth Korea had the largest classes at both levels, with 34 pupils in primary and 36 in lower secondary schools, well above the world averages of 25 and 28 respectively. Japan came second, at 29 and 34. Mexico's primary classes were the smallest of all, at 20 pupils, but its lower secondary classes were half as large again, at 30.\n\nIn the UK, by contrast, primary classes averaged 27 pupils, slightly more than the global figure, whereas lower secondary classes had only 18, the lowest number on the chart. Denmark and Iceland had similar class sizes at both levels, ranging from 20 to 23 pupils, which was below the world average in every case.","lv":"in","pn":["South Korea","Mexico","Denmark"]},
{"id":"d1nIXx","tp":"bar","d":["2019-01"],"q":"The chart below shows waste collection by a recycling centre from 2011 to 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Waste collected by a recycling centre, 2011–2015 (tons of waste)","unit":"Tons","x":["2011","2012","2013","2014","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Paper","v":[57,50,40,51,70]},{"n":"Glass","v":[48,41,48,46,52]},{"n":"Tins","v":[35,27,34,33,39]},{"n":"Garden","v":[32,15,31,27,35]}]},"mo":"The bar chart shows how many tons of paper, glass, tins and garden waste were collected by a recycling centre each year from 2011 to 2015.\n\nOverall, paper was the most collected material in four of the five years, and garden waste the least in every year. Although collections of all four materials dipped during the period, each ended at its highest level in 2015.\n\nIn 2011, the centre collected 57 tons of paper, 48 tons of glass, 35 tons of tins and 32 tons of garden waste. All four figures fell the following year, with garden waste more than halving to 15 tons. Paper continued to decline, reaching a low of 40 tons in 2013, when it was overtaken by glass, which had returned to 48 tons.\n\nFrom 2013 onwards, the amount of paper rose sharply, climbing to 51 tons in 2014 and 70 tons in 2015, almost twice the figure for tins. Glass, by contrast, stayed within a narrow range and finished at 52 tons, while tins and garden waste recovered from their 2012 lows to 39 and 35 tons respectively.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1DNgRi","tp":"bar","d":["2018-12"],"q":"The bar chart below shows the gross earnings from sales of five types of fiction book between 2006 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Fiction book sales: gross earnings, 2006–2010 ($)","unit":"$","x":["2006","2007","2008","2009","2010"],"series":[{"n":"Young Adult","v":[31000,31000,19000,25000,28000]},{"n":"Classics","v":[17000,31000,22000,26000,10000]},{"n":"Mystery","v":[61000,82000,53000,49000,55000]},{"n":"Romance","v":[83000,114000,71000,78000,86000]},{"n":"Sci-Fi & Fantasy","v":[17000,20000,14000,11000,15000]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the gross earnings from sales of five types of fiction, young adult, classics, mystery, romance and science fiction and fantasy, each year from 2006 to 2010.\n\nOverall, romance was the best-selling genre in every year, followed by mystery, while the other three categories earned far less. Most genres had their best year in 2007, and most earned slightly less in 2010 than they had at the start.\n\nRomance generated about $83,000 in 2006 and rose to a peak of roughly $114,000 the following year, before dropping to $71,000 in 2008 and then recovering to $86,000. Mystery followed a similar pattern at a lower level, climbing from $61,000 to $82,000 in 2007 and ending at $55,000, roughly two-thirds of the romance figure.\n\nEarnings from the other genres never exceeded $31,000. Young adult fiction earned this amount in the first two years, fell to $19,000 in 2008 and finished at $28,000. Classics almost doubled to match it in 2007 but had sunk to just $10,000 by 2010, the lowest figure on the chart, whereas science fiction and fantasy stayed between $11,000 and $20,000 throughout.","lv":"in","rc":1},
{"id":"d1LXu8","tp":"bar","d":["2018-08"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage shares of three sectors (agriculture, industry and services) in one country between 1960 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Shares of agriculture, industry and services in one country, 1960–2000 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1960","1970","1980","1990","2000"],"series":[{"n":"Agriculture","v":[63,54,46,31,14]},{"n":"Industry","v":[16,21,25,25,25]},{"n":"Services","v":[17,24,28,45,63]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi và không ghi đơn vị; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình. Cô nên đối chiếu với đề gốc."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage shares of agriculture, industry and services in one country at ten-year intervals between 1960 and 2000.\n\nOverall, the country shifted away from agriculture and towards services over the forty years. Agriculture went from being by far the largest sector to the smallest, whereas services moved in the opposite direction, and industry grew modestly before levelling off.\n\nIn 1960, agriculture represented 63% of the total, almost four times the share of either industry (16%) or services (17%). Its share then fell in every decade, to 54% in 1970 and 46% in 1980, before dropping more steeply to 31% in 1990 and just 14% in 2000, less than a quarter of the original figure.\n\nServices, by contrast, expanded throughout the period. Having risen gradually to 28% by 1980, the sector overtook agriculture during the following decade, reaching 45% in 1990 and 63% in 2000, exactly the share that agriculture had held forty years earlier. Industry increased from 16% to 25% between 1960 and 1980 and stayed at that level thereafter.","lv":"pre","rc":1},
{"id":"d1LPgzC","tp":"bar","d":["2018-06"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of patients in three hospital wards who replied 'very good' about four aspects of their hospital experience. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Patients who replied 'very good' about their hospital experience, by ward (%)","unit":"%","x":["Hospital staff","Waiting time","Quality of treatment","Quality of aftercare"],"series":[{"n":"Holly Ward","v":[87,75,90,88]},{"n":"Maple Ward","v":[55,55,72,52]},{"n":"Oak Ward","v":[68,53,80,70]}],"note":"Ward: phòng hoặc khu dành cho một nhóm bệnh nhân. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of patients in three hospital wards, Holly, Maple and Oak, who rated four aspects of their stay as 'very good'.\n\nOverall, Holly Ward received the highest ratings for every aspect, while Maple Ward was rated lowest for three of the four. In all three wards, quality of treatment was the most highly rated aspect, and waiting time was generally among the least satisfactory.\n\nIn Holly Ward, around nine in ten patients gave the top rating to the quality of treatment (90%), aftercare (88%) and hospital staff (87%). Even its weakest area, waiting time, satisfied 75% of patients, more than in any other ward.\n\nOak Ward came second overall. Its treatment was rated very good by 80% of patients, compared with 70% for aftercare and 68% for staff, whereas only 53% were happy with the waiting time. Maple Ward scored 72% for treatment, but just over half of its patients gave the top rating to the other three aspects, with figures of 55% for both staff and waiting time and 52% for aftercare, which was 36 percentage points below the figure for Holly Ward.","lv":"in","pn":["Holly","Maple","Oak"]},
{"id":"d1mGyh2","tp":"bar","d":["2018-01"],"q":"The chart below shows the favourite leisure activities of teenagers in Canada. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Favourite leisure activities of teenagers in Canada (%)","unit":"%","x":["Sports","Computer games","Music","Shopping"],"series":[{"n":"Boys","v":[27,18,20,35]},{"n":"Girls","v":[12,15,20,7]}],"note":"Số liệu và chú giải chép đúng theo hình trong bộ đề; cô nên đối chiếu với đề gốc."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the favourite leisure activities of teenage boys and girls in Canada, comparing the percentages who prefer sports, computer games, music and shopping.\n\nOverall, the figures for boys are higher than or equal to those for girls in all four categories. The difference is greatest for shopping and sports, whereas music is equally popular with both sexes.\n\nAccording to the chart, shopping ranks first among boys, chosen by 35%, five times the proportion of girls (7%). Sports come second for boys at 27%, more than twice the figure for girls, which is 12%. Together, these two activities are the first choice of 62% of boys.\n\nThe two sexes are far closer in their liking for the other activities. Music is the favourite of exactly 20% of both boys and girls, which makes it the most popular choice among girls. Computer games attract 18% of boys and 15% of girls, a difference of only three percentage points. For girls, therefore, the order of preference is music, computer games, sports and shopping, while for boys it is almost the reverse.","lv":"in","rc":1},
{"id":"d1vrT36","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The bar chart below shows the percentage of Australian men and women in different age groups who did regular physical activity in 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Percentage of Australian men and women doing regular physical activity, 2010","unit":"%","x":["15 to 24","25 to 34","35 to 44","45 to 54","55 to 64","65 and over"],"series":[{"n":"Male","v":[52.8,42.2,39.5,43.1,45.1,46.7]},{"n":"Female","v":[47.7,48.9,52.5,53.3,53,47.1]}]},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of Australian men and women in six age groups who did regular physical activity in 2010.\n\nOverall, women were more active than men in every age group except the youngest. Male participation was lowest in early middle age, whereas among women it was highest in the middle-aged groups.\n\nIn the youngest group, aged 15 to 24, 52.8% of men exercised regularly, compared with 47.7% of women. This was the only group in which men led, and it was also the highest male figure on the chart. Male participation then dropped in the next two age groups, reaching a low of 39.5% among those aged 35 to 44, before recovering gradually to 46.7% in the oldest group.\n\nFemale participation followed the opposite pattern. It rose with age to a peak of 53.3% among women aged 45 to 54 and was almost as high in the groups on either side. The gap between the sexes was therefore widest in early middle age, at 13 percentage points, while among people aged 65 and over the two figures were virtually identical, at around 47%.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 12 · Test 1"},
{"id":"d1dYd9x","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The chart below shows how frequently people in the USA ate in fast food restaurants between 2003 and 2013. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Frequency of eating at fast food restaurants among people in the USA, 2003–2013 (% of people)","unit":"% of people","x":["Every day","Several times a week","Once a week","Once or twice a month","A few times a year","Never"],"series":[{"n":"2003","v":[4,17,31,30,13,5]},{"n":"2006","v":[3,20,33,25,15,4]},{"n":"2013","v":[3,16,28,33,15,4]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows how often people in the USA ate at fast food restaurants in 2003, 2006 and 2013.\n\nOverall, the majority of Americans ate fast food between once a week and once or twice a month in all three years, while very few ate it every day or never. Over the decade, eating fast food weekly or more often became slightly less common.\n\nIn 2003, 31% of people visited a fast food restaurant once a week and 30% did so once or twice a month. The weekly figure rose to 33% in 2006 but dropped to 28% in 2013, whereas the monthly figure moved in the opposite direction, falling to 25% before climbing to 33%, the highest share that year. Similarly, the proportion eating fast food several times a week peaked at 20% in 2006 and then declined to 16%.\n\nAt the two extremes, only 3 to 4% of respondents ate fast food daily, and roughly the same proportion never did. Those who went a few times a year made up 13% in 2003 and 15% in both later years.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 12 · Test 3"},
{"id":"d1AzI6w","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of households in owned and rented accommodation in England and Wales between 1918 and 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Households owning and renting accommodation in England and Wales, 1918 to 2011 (% of households)","unit":"% of households","x":["1918","1939","1953","1961","1971","1981","1991","2001","2011"],"series":[{"n":"Households in owned accommodation","v":[23,32,32,41,50,60,68,69,64]},{"n":"Households in rented accommodation","v":[77,68,68,59,50,40,32,31,36]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of households in England and Wales living in owned and in rented accommodation in nine selected years between 1918 and 2011.\n\nOverall, home ownership rose substantially over the period, while renting declined to a similar extent. Renting was far more common until 1971, when the two were equal, after which owning became the majority arrangement.\n\nIn 1918, 77% of households rented their homes, more than three times the proportion that owned them (23%). The share of owners rose to 32% by 1939 and, after staying at that level until 1953, climbed to 41% in 1961 and exactly half in 1971, with renting falling correspondingly.\n\nFrom 1971 onwards, the positions were reversed. Ownership continued to grow, rising to 60% in 1981 and a peak of 69% in 2001, by which time fewer than a third of households were renting. The final decade saw a slight change of direction, however: the proportion of owner-occupiers dropped to 64% in 2011, whereas that of renters rose to 36%, the first increase in renting in almost a century.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 13 · Test 2"},
{"id":"d1mmo4s","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The bar chart below shows the top ten countries for the production and consumption of electricity in 2014. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Top ten countries for production and consumption of electricity, 2014 (billion kWh)","unit":"Billion kWh","x":["China","United States","Russia","Japan","India","Canada","France","Brazil","Germany","Korea, Rep."],"series":[{"n":"Production","v":[5398,4099,1057,936.2,871,618.9,561.2,530.7,526.6,485.1]},{"n":"Consumption","v":[5322,3866,1038,856.7,698.8,499.9,462.9,455.8,582.5,449.5]}]},"mo":"The bar chart ranks the ten countries that produced and consumed the most electricity in 2014, measured in billions of kilowatt-hours.\n\nOverall, China and the United States generated and used far more electricity than any other country, with a very large gap separating them from the rest. Almost every country in the list produced slightly more electricity than it consumed, the only exception being Germany.\n\nChina led the ranking, producing 5,398 billion kWh and consuming 5,322 billion. The United States came second, with production of 4,099 billion kWh and consumption of 3,866 billion. Together, these two countries produced more than all the other eight combined.\n\nRussia, in third place, generated 1,057 billion kWh, roughly a quarter of the American total, and Japan and India followed with 936.2 and 871 billion respectively. The remaining five countries, Canada, France, Brazil, Germany and South Korea, each produced between about 485 and 620 billion kWh. Germany was unusual in that its consumption, at 582.5 billion kWh, exceeded its production of 526.6 billion, whereas India and Canada had the largest surpluses relative to their output.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 13 · Test 3"},
{"id":"d1Vl91g","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The chart below shows the results of a survey about people's coffee and tea buying and drinking habits in five Australian cities. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Coffee and tea buying and drinking habits in five cities in Australia (% of city residents)","unit":"% of city residents","x":["Sydney","Melbourne","Brisbane","Adelaide","Hobart"],"series":[{"n":"Bought fresh coffee in last 4 weeks","v":[44,42.5,34.5,34.5,38.5]},{"n":"Bought instant coffee in last 4 weeks","v":[45.5,48,52.5,50,54.5]},{"n":"Went to a café for coffee or tea in last 4 weeks","v":[61,63.5,55.5,49.5,63]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of residents in five Australian cities who had bought fresh coffee, bought instant coffee or visited a café for coffee or tea in the previous four weeks.\n\nOverall, going to a café was the most common of the three activities in every city except Adelaide, while buying fresh coffee was the least common everywhere. Instant coffee was bought by more people than fresh coffee in all five cities.\n\nCafé visits were most widespread in Melbourne and Hobart, where around 63% of residents had been to one, closely followed by Sydney at 61%. The figures were lower in Brisbane (roughly 55%) and Adelaide, where just under half of residents had visited a café, marginally fewer than had bought instant coffee.\n\nPurchases of instant coffee ranged from about 45% in Sydney to 54% in Hobart. Fresh coffee, by contrast, was most popular in Sydney and Melbourne, at 44% and 42% respectively, whereas only about 34% of people in Brisbane and Adelaide had bought it. Sydney was therefore the only city in which the two types of coffee were bought by almost equal proportions of residents.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 15 · Test 1","pn":["Sydney","Melbourne","Hobart","Brisbane","Adelaide"]},
{"id":"d1ceA9x","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The chart below gives information about how families in one country spent their weekly income in 1968 and in 2018. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"1968 and 2018: average weekly spending by families (% of weekly income)","unit":"% of weekly income","x":["Food","Housing","Fuel and power","Clothing and footwear","Household goods","Personal goods","Transport","Leisure"],"series":[{"n":"1968","v":[35,10,6,10,8,8,8,9]},{"n":"2018","v":[17,19,4,5,8,4,14,22]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the proportions of weekly income that families in one country spent on eight categories in 1968 and in 2018.\n\nOverall, the share of income devoted to food fell markedly, whereas spending on housing, transport and leisure took up considerably more of the family budget. Food was by far the largest expense in 1968, but leisure had overtaken it fifty years later.\n\nIn 1968, families spent 35% of their weekly income on food, more than three times the share for any other category. By 2018, this figure had halved to 17%. The proportions spent on clothing and footwear and on personal goods also halved, from 10% to 5% and from 8% to 4% respectively, while fuel and power declined slightly to 4%.\n\nBy contrast, leisure rose from 9% to 22%, making it the largest item in 2018. Housing almost doubled, from 10% to 19%, and transport climbed from 8% to 14%. Household goods were the only category to show no change, accounting for 8% of income in both years.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 17 · Test 3"},
{"id":"d1lZnco","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The chart below shows the number of households in the US by their annual income in 2007, 2011 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Number of US households (in millions), by annual income","unit":"Millions","x":["2007","2011","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Less than $25,000","v":[25.3,29,28]},{"n":"$25,000–$49,999","v":[27,30,28.8]},{"n":"$50,000–$74,999","v":[21,21.2,21]},{"n":"$75,000–$99,999","v":[14.5,14,15.2]},{"n":"$100,000 or more","v":[29.5,28,33.5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the number of households in the United States in five annual income brackets in 2007, 2011 and 2015.\n\nOverall, the two lowest and the highest income brackets contained the most households in all three years, while the second-highest bracket contained the fewest. The top bracket saw the largest growth over the period, becoming clearly the biggest group by 2015.\n\nIn 2007, 29.5 million households earned $100,000 or more, slightly ahead of the 27 million in the second-lowest bracket and the 25 million earning less than $25,000. By 2011, the two lower groups had each grown by about 3 to 4 million, overtaking the top bracket, which had slipped to 28 million. This was reversed in 2015, when the number of the richest households rose to roughly 33.5 million, while both lower-income groups declined slightly.\n\nThe middle brackets changed far less. About 21 million households fell into the third bracket in every year, and the second-highest group was consistently the smallest, at 14 to 15 million, roughly half the size of the top bracket.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 18 · Test 2"}
];
